Food to Blame for European Inflation
The main reason for the rise in inflation measured in the eurozone is the rapidly rising food prices.
In the 13 countries of the eurozone, inflation was higher than expected in November - according to the 2.6 per cent in October, an annual inflation rate of 3.1 per cent was registered, according to the EU's statistical office in Luxembourg.
Official forecasts arose of just 3 percent, Bloomberg reported.
In contrast to the American, British, and Canadian central banks, the European Central Bank (ECB) has not lowered the key interest rate interest rate interest rate. According to their discretion, the rate cut would favour rising commodity prices and the slight drop in unemployment.
The ECB is eager to keep the rate of inflation below two per cent, or at least narrowly at the two-per cent mark.
According to an analyst, the ECB is in a quandary: inflation must be "dedicated" in order to rule out the need for later action.
At 3.3 per cent, the inflation rate reached a twelve-year record high in Germany in November; in Spain, the rate climbed from 3.6 to 4.1 percent.
Increased food and energy prices are responsible for the rise in inflation in the eurozone.
MATIF wheat quotes rose 88 percent, and soybean prices also hit a record high since 1973.
There was also a significant increase in edible oil and dairy products in 2007.
The world market price for crude oil rose 52 percent this year - last month, the price per barrel of black gold reached nearly US$100.
According to the ECB, inflation will rise from 2.1 to 2.5 percent in 2008, but it is expected to decline to 1.9 percent in 2009.
For the next 3-4 months, analysts assume a 3 percent inflation rate, but estimate the annual average rate at 2.1 percent.
For 2008, experts reckon the ECB will cut interest rates twice.
Gallup locates government crisis
After much stagnation, the number of Fidesz sympathizers has grown considerably by the beginning of December. For the first time since 2002, the party has the largest base base camp, while approval for the socialists has reached an unprecedented low of 13 percent, according to Gallup.
Among party-loyal voters, the difference is clear thanks to the high determination of opposition supporters: more than two-third (71 percent) face one-fifth (20 percent) approval for the MSZP.
MDF and SZDSZ, according to the poll, are below the parliamentary entry hurdle: They come in the overall population to two percent.
The number of party voters has grown by one percent in both camps.
Compared to the last low, overall economic sentiment has deteriorated further - never before was the proportion of those who judge the country's economic situation as "extremely bad" so high (41 per cent), while another 46 per cent consider it "pretty bad."
According to more hopeful prospects in spring and summer, the families see the change in their financial situation again more pessimistic.
For the coming year, the majority of the population expects a further deterioration of the situation; only one-tenth assume an improvement.
A Gallup poll conducted in December found that 71% of respondents - a historic high - judged the prime minister's work to be "rather bad than right."
Thirteen percent of the respondents generally have great confidence or confidence in Ferenc Gyurcsány, while the proportion of proponents for Viktor Orbán represents 38 percent.
Around 54% don't trust the head of government at all, compared to 27% distrust of Fidesz's party leader.
One of the indicators of political sentiment is the number of those who think you could get to grips with dissatisfaction with the prime minister even without a change of government.
After the riots last October, 35% were still in favour of the government and the head-of-governmen, but today this proportion amounts to only 20%, while the relative majority (33 percent) see the solution only in new elections.
Asked about their interest in the popular vote, 60 percent replied that they "certainately" participate, and another 20% would "probably" cast their vote.
By comparison, if the parliamentary elections were to take place this Sunday, only 55 percent would "certainately" go to the polls, another 15% consider their participation to be "likely". Given these tendencies, the result of the referendum would be foreseeable.
74% would vote against tuition fees, 79% against the practice fee, and 84% against the hospital daily allowance.
Encircled to the group of "certainly" participants in the referendum, the shares of the opponents of the study, practice and hospital fees would even be 81, 84, or 89 percent.
Auchan the most expensive, Tesco increased by almost a fifth
The hypermarkets in the Budaörs trading area have also this year maintained the average price increase of 16% from last year, with the difference that there was no increase in VAT this year.
Articles from Napi Gazdaság
In Budaör's trading triangle, the price of the usual Christmas basket of the "Napi" has also increased by 16 percent this year, but price increases of more than 1,000 HUF have been registered in all hypermarkets.
After the prices in the food markets "galloped away" last December, the price increase has not been drastic, compared to the already high, earlier starting level.
However, it certainly deterrently affects consumers that what they had still paid for far below HUF 9000 last year is not even available for 10,000 HUF this year.
The price of "Napi" shopping cart exceeded the magical 10,000 HUF limit by at least 91 HUF in the examined markets.
The highest price was 10,573 HUF.
(In 6800 the price was 6,800 HUF.)
The prices for Unicum and vibrant fish remained almost at the previous year’s level, while the prices for turkey breast fillets, nuts and poppies rose sharply.
Prices for flour and bread corresponded to the data of the Central Statistical Office of Hungary, but at Auchan there were 1kg oils of bread for 139 forints.
There was no competition-related price fall to below 200 forints.
Surprisingly there was also the price of a two liter Coca-Cola deposit bottle of less than 200 forints, so the drink, including deposit, cost only 257 HUF.
Still, this year the "Napi" scorted shopping cart comparison Auchan the most expensive, and Cora the cheapest.
Our calculations have revealed that Cora have increased the prices in "Napi" shopping cart by 15 compared to Christmas prices last year, while Tesco and Auchan have each increased by 18 percent.
The difference between last year's cheapest shopping cart and today's most expensive was 12%, while the increase between last year's cheapest shopping cart and the most expensive of the current year amounted to 21%.
The average price increase was 1,480.00 HUF.
The average price increase in the previous year was 1,206.00 forints, but the VAT increase was noticeable in terms of prices at last Christmas.
Exactly this process has hit the trade badly: apparently, the department stores could not quite decide which price range they should apply to the individual items, since the effects of the price increase on consumer behaviour were unforeseeable.
(If the trader poorly plans the circulating speed of a product, he may fall for significant gains in the price range, which depends on the traffic intensity.)
Due to VAT, the inflation of the "Napi" shopping cart last year was so radical that the most expensive basket exceeded the value of 2006 around 1720 forints, whereas the price difference for the cheapest basket was only 844 forints.
This year, such extremes were not observed, as the most expensive basket by 1,630.00 forint cost more than in the previous year, and even with the cheapest, there was a price increase of 1,284.00 forints.
Compared to the last two Christmas seasons, a catastrophic price increase showed, the price of daily consumables in the hypermarkets has risen by more than 30 percent in less than 20 months.
(This, in turn, does not necessarily confirm the data of the Central Statistical Office of Hungary: for some staple foodstuffs, the Statistical Office noticed a more than 30 percent increase over the period of 12 months.
In addition to flour also with eggs.)
However, if you take a look at the "Napi" shopping cart, this seems almost impossible for the latter, since the price level of the product, which was already expensive in the previous year, has risen by barely more than 10 percent, even in the worst case.
Clearly, flour suppliers are in a stronger position than the egg suppliers - after all, they were able to pass the drastic price increase onto the multinationals.
In the golden trading triangle, the hypermarkets do not complain; it is constantly reported of good years.
Our experience shows that the majority of customers in the three department stores no longer look at the prices.
Large quantities are shopped out of everything, durable groceries go in pallets over the counters.
Wednesday morning in the morning there were endless queues in all three department stores, when suddenly the number of customers exploded.
However, a lower customer frequency is reported by the country, so that many multinationals complain to the pollsters that they could not even reach the planned turnover, not only for food, but also for sustainable consumer goods.
In many places, disinfectants are saved
More than a thousand infections have been registered this year in hospitals due to improper handling of antibiotics.
The hygienic conditions in the hospitals, the overcrowding of the beds, the overload of the staff - all factors that are not exactly conducive to the containment of infections, according to the leading doctor of the National Epidemiology Center.
In many places, disinfectants are also saved.
An MRSA infection has recently been diagnosed in the Traumatology Department of the János Hospital.
The bacterium, resistant to antibiotics, has been found in a male patient who was hospitalized after a serious accident.
The patient has been isolated.
At the beginning of the year there was already a similar infection here.
The injured of a serious car accident was discharged from the hospital, but a little later the man had to return with an MRSA infection. An important operation could not yet be carried out since because of the bacterium, which is still in the patient's body.
By December 10 of this year, 1094 infections triggered by multidrugent pathogens were reported to the National Nosocomialis Surveillance System, according to National Epidemiology Center senior physician Karolina Böröcz.
So many infections triggered by multi-resistant pathogens have certainly been registered in the hospitals, but the dark figure is likely to be higher.
Approximately two-thirds of infections (683 cases) were triggered by MRSA, Staphylococcus aureus resistant to methicillin oxacillin.
Staphylococcus aureus (SA) bacterium carries about one-third of people in their noses without causing a disease.
SA-related infections (such as sore throat, tonsillitis, inflammation of the skin or wounds) usually respond well to antibiotics, they are easily curable.
However, if the agents are metered too high or applied improperly, antibiotic-resistants arise from the well-responsive bacteria, which spread rapidly under appropriate conditions in the hospitals.
Especially in patients weakened, or permanently poorly functioning immune systems, but also in the elderly, or patients with low body weight, these bacteria can cause serious infections, such as wound infection or blood poisoning, i.e. sepsis.
According to the medical director of the János hospital, the MRSA infection is nothing special, such incidents are part of everyday hospital life.
One should not confuse the infection with the epidemy, warned Tibor Nyulasi - MRSA only does not exist where it is not searched.
Resistant to antibiotics
"Such patients are isolated, the nursing staff wear rubber gloves and masks during care, and we have the antibiotics necessary for treatment," according to Tibor Nyulasi.
When admitted to the hospital, patients are examined for MRSA, but an infection cannot always be avoided.
Sampling too rarely
The majority of the infections triggered by MRSA in hospitals would be avoidable, but the currently prevailing conditions and circumstances are unfavorable for the containment of the development or spread of the multi-resistant pathogen, says Karolina Böröcz.
Since this infection occurs predominantly in hospitals and retirement homes, the hospitals should examine the admitted patients and isolate MRSA-infected people immediately.
However, microbiological sampling is not practiced as frequently in Hungarian hospitals.
Not least because there are no subsidies for this investigation on the part of the state health insurance company.
Most cases would therefore only come to light when the patient already has a noticeable MRSA infection, according to the medical director of the epidemiology center.
There are strict recommendations on the part of the European Union for the appropriate use of antibiotics in human medicine, but there are also guidelines for the treatment of certain infections and clinical pictures in Hungary.
Antibiotic therapies should be targeted, i.e. microbiological examinations are necessary so that no resistance to the agent can form, but this directive is not always adhered to by hospitals.
Microbiological sampling is even rarer among the outpatients, or in primary care, Karolina Böröcz said.
Savings are made on disinfectants
Scientific evidence proves that MRSA is most often transmitted by nursing staff from one patient to the other.
Precisely for this reason, hand washing or wearing gloves and masks is of great importance, but there are also significant shortcomings in the hospitals in this area.
MRSA is transmitted by droplet infection or contact, but there is not always the possibility of isolating the infected patient in the domestic hospitals.
The rooms are even often overcrowded, the beds lined close to each other, although there should be a distance of at least 1.5 meters between them from an epidemiological point of view.
As Karolina Böröcz further notes, it unfortunately even happens in the financially less well-off hospitals that disinfectants are saved, there are few caregivers, many patients - all factors that promote the development of infections.
Such infections are demonstrably far less frequent there, or the further spread of the pathogen can be stopped, where these protective measures are adhered to, according to Karolina Böröcz.
Government programme against MRSA
Getaring MRSA infections undercount the health insurance companies causes enormous costs, so they are combated everywhere.
In England, for example, the prevention of MRSA, or its containment, has been recorded in the government programme.
After initial signs, the programme is successful - as published data show, the trend of MRSA infections in England is declining.
The European Centre for Disease Prevention (ECDC) has declared the prevention of the development of multidrugent pathogens (including MRSA), as well as the prevention of infections in hospitals as one of its priorities.
Death driver's police chief wasn't drunk
In the case of the car accident caused by the police chief of the XXII district with a fatal outcome, there is now the official report.
The now-resigned police chief of XXII's district, who killed a pedestrian in a car accident on Tuesday, was neither alcoholic nor under the influence of drugs, Attila Morvai, the press secretary of the Budapest Supreme Prosecutor's Office, said on Friday, after the official opinion on the case was submitted.
The opinion confirmed the results of the initial investigation, according to which the police chief had not consumed any alcohol before getting behind the wheel.
The former police chief turned into Kossuth Lajo's street with his own car in XXII's district and ran over a 55-year-old man who crossed the road at an unmarked spot.
(According to StVO, however, this is not a mitigating circumstance - the handlebars, even if pedestrians do not cross the road at the zebra crossing, sub-ranking .)
The pedestrian succumbed to his injuries after being admitted to the hospital.
According to police reports, the handlebars suffered a shock, then put back his functions and asked for retirement.
The Budapest prosecutor's office has launched its investigation into the incident.
Attila Morvai also mentioned that the former police chief had cooperated with the investigators from the beginning.
An additional 400 million euros for infrastructure
Finance Minister János Veres and Marta Gajecka, vice-president of the European Investment Bank (EIB) signed three loan agreements totalling 400 million euros in Budapest on Friday. The money is to flow into education, transport and environmental protection infrastructure and the energy industry.
The European Investment Bank (EIB) approved a 300 million EUR framework for the financing of transport and environmental protection projects implemented with grants from the Cohesion Fund in 2006. The loan agreement for 100 million has already been signed earlier, the current agreement deals with the use of the 200 million euros awarded.
The sum agreed in the loan agreement of 150 million euros is also to be expended on the budgetary financing of educational projects.
The EIB has approved a total of 300 million euros for Hungary for this purpose; the use of the remaining 150 million euros will be decided later on as part of the extension of the lending agreement.
In the area of innovation, the contracting parties have signed an agreement of 50 million euros, which will mainly flow into research and development programmes at the University of Debrecen.
At the meeting, metro line 4 was also discussed.
It has been agreed that as soon as Hungary clarifies the details of subway financing with the EU, the original contract and eventual modifications will be discussed again.
According to János Veres, this would be possible in the first quarter of 2008.
Since 1990, the EIB has financed a total of 113 projects totalling 8.4 billion euros in Hungary.
The borrowing rates are EURIBOR-linked, the loan is provided by the EIB with a deferral of 8 years, commission-free.
Bulagus, the real estate paradise of the EU
The development of the Bulgarian real estate market is fabulous - the whole of Europe looks attentively at the formerly rarely mentioned Balkan state.
What, where, and who buys in the EU’s still cheapest market, but which is becoming rapidly more expensive?
An analysis of the Bulgarian Re/Max Division.
Bulgaria is currently undoubtedly the most sought-after pavement for property purchases in Eastern Europe, with cheap ski apartments and houses by the sea.
Between the mountains, the regions of Bansko, Borovec, Pamprovo and Vitosa are among the most popular, by the sea are Albena, the "Gold Coast", the "Sun Coast", and the historical towns of Sozopol and Nessebar, but real estate around Kavarna and Balcik is also increasingly in demand.
According to Re/Max analysts in Bulgaria, the inner country will sooner or later attract investors, for example Veliko Tarnovo, Gabrovo, Elena, and Triavna, and also the decidedly cheap area of Russe records more buyers.
The capital Sofia also offers tempting offerings - commercial and office space offers the highest returns.
Bulgaria is on the best way to becoming one of the most attractive European golfer paradises, and thus, in addition to the summer and winter season, also attract tourists in the other two seasons.
Anticipatory investors are therefore already shopping on a large scale, for example around Bansko.
According to analysts, people who acquire real estate in Bulgaria are also seeking a change in their lifestyle.
Most buyers are English, but there are also interested parties from France, Belgium, Spain, and more and more from Romania.
Investors from Malta and Cyprus are also present, while there are predominantly Russian buyers at the Black Sea and in the ski resorts.
There are three groups of foreign buyers, says Georgij Jankov, regional director of Re/Max Bulgaria.
Those who want to settle buy either revitalized properties around 50-100,000 euros, or run-down houses for 5-15,000 euros, which they then renovate themselves.
Good connections and accessibility as well as communication options are very important, but also the proximity to a large city, shops and doctors are crucial for the buyers.
The second category includes those who are looking for a holiday home (Bulgaria was listed in December among the top ten holiday destinations of the Germans).
The third group make up business people who are investing with the goal of a medium to long-term increase in value.
Properties in the capital, meanwhile, are constantly becoming more expensive, due to the ever-increasing demand.
Within the EU, property prices are the lowest in Bulgaria, but in the past five years there has been a price increase of 21 percent annually.
The increase in property prices far exceeds the increase in salaries.
Fodor and the six Hungarian percent
Environment Minister G�ábor Fodor announced that Tuesday's expert meetings are coming to an end, and the debate about the international climate regime after 2012 will continue by the ministers on Wednesday after the official opening.
Experts and politicians from 192 countries are discussinging the Bali roadmap, in which emission-reducing measures are defined for the period after 2012, i.e. after the Kyoto Protocol has expired.
There is currently a fierce debate going on between developed and developing countries.
The U.S. has already signalled that it does not want to negotiate on concrete figures, but they would be willing to reduce greenhouse gas emissions by 25-40 percent by 2020 compared to 1990.
The other big question of the conference is whether the industrialized countries are prepared to support developing countries both financially and technologically in addition to reducing emissions.
An agreement on support for developing countries has also been reached.
The Adaptation Fund subordinated to the Kyoto Protocol is to be managed by a 16-member Council, probably at the UN's Global Environment Facility (GEF), as can be seen in the "Piac és Profit".
Hungary's six percent
Environment Minister Gábor Fodor, on behalf of Hungary, supports the EU's aspiration to maintain its pioneering role in global climate protection measures and to reach a new international agreement by 2009.
The minister stated that the EU will reduce greenhouse gas emissions by 20% by 2020 (measured by 1990) regardless of whether or not the "post-2012 agreement" will be achieved. The Union would even be willing to work for a 30% reduction, provided that other major industrialized countries in the world or developing countries are also willing to meet their obligations to the best and responsibly.
Gábor Fodor emphasized that the objective of the countries that joined the Kyoto Protocol in 1997 was a reduction of only 5.2 percent, whereas the EU, together with its then 15 member states, had already committed itself to a minus of 8%.
The requirement for Hungary is to reduce greenhouse gas emissions by 6% compared to the years 1985-87 - a requirement that the country will certainly meet, as can be found in the press release of the Ministry of the Environment.
The deployment is high in Bali - after all, the long-term international climate policy agreement must be ready within two years, otherwise it would certainly only come into force after 2012. In this case, the reduction projects set out in the Kyoto Protocol would not be followed by a renewed, international emissions reduction system whose importance has also been highlighted by the IPCC, the UN's scientific climate change committee awarded with the Nobel Peace Prize.
As a result, the emission reduction would be delayed, and the increase in Earth's average temperature by more than 2°C would be inevitable, resulting in extremely serious and irreparable consequences, according to scientists.
British army advertises on YouTube
Units of the British army have published recordings about operations in Iraq and Afghanistan to increase popularity on the video portal.
At first, the British Air Force (RAF) has discovered YouTube for itself: special forces stationed in Afghanistan have been uploading videos of Taliban-leaded operations on the net since March 2006, the BBC reports.
The footage shot with miniature cameras attached on helmets is checked at the air base in Kandahar and then sent to London, where they are placed on the Internet.
Political message unavoidable
Air Force soldiers have already contributed 139 such shots, their "canal" has been viewed more than 75,000 times.
Britain's Ministry of Defense was registered under "defenceheadquarts" in the spring of 2007 to publish videos about Iraqi and Afghan operations, but also the British Foreign Office (Commonwealth Office) and international peacekeepers (MNFI) stationed in Iraq have their own YouTube channels.
Deployment in Afghanistan
Originally, the Air Force released the videos with the aim of recruiting soldiers, and as the Ministry of Defense says, the contributions also serve the purpose of informing the population about the actual objectives of foreign missions.
Propaganda published in this way is almost free of charge compared to conventional television advertisements, according to defense expert Gordon Mackenzie to the BBC, but these videos always carry a certain degree of political embassy, as they confirm the justification for existence of the military measures decided by the government in their own way.
Advocating Americans with game
However, the army must also fight against amateur recordings, which, in addition to the carefully processed, censored shots - such as those that show the deaths of the soldiers: In the somewhat hypocritical, "official" propaganda films there is at most talk about the losses, but the bloody sides of the war are not shown.
Incidentally, from the theatres of war in Iraq and Afghanistan, numerous videos filmed in use have already been uploaded to the platform, but the British have only recently discovered this "genus".
The Americans, meanwhile, advertise in the slightly different way, namely with the free downloadable shooting game "America's Army", in which the player can slip into the skin of the soldier as part of a more or less life-scale simulation.
Christmas party with raffle and pogates in ministries
Labour Minister Mónika Lamperth is by no means squeamish: instead of a Christmas party, she organizes a working conference in which her employees can end the year 2007 with coffee and pogash.
Some ministries celebrate with live music, others conjure up Christmas atmosphere with raffles or toasts to the official hearts.
Party, Christmas mood, concerts and buffet - that's how power celebrates.
Christmas fever has already gripped offices as well.
The largest Christmas party of a ministry will be held this year in the defense department, but the employees of the ministry are not expected.
At the "Christmas celebration of the orphans" children are gifted by fallen soldiers, at the "mission celebration" family members of soldiers currently stationed abroad are hosted, but there is also a reception for the press.
The department provides around 25 million for the celebrations.
Compared to other departments, the National Development Agency (NFU) and the Ministry of Economy and Transport are taking over.
The 4.32-million-forint Christmas party of the Ministry of Economic Affairs will be held at the House of the future, with about 350 of the 600 employees expected.
The focus of the festivities is on Christmas, but there will also be live acts and a raffle raffle.
The National Agency for Development (NFÜ) held its 4.7-million-forint Christmas party on Wednesday at the House of the State Justice Council (OIT), with concerts and a festive dinner.
The room rent amounted to 1 milion forints, the rest was spent on the more than 400 invited guests.
According to our information, one department of the NFÜ is also invited at another Christmas party, which is organized by the Hungarian Economic Development Center (MAG Zrt) from EU funds.
For the celebration, the building of Lustspieltheater ("Vígszínház") was rented.
MAG Zrt has not confirmed this information because we have not reached those responsible.
Why the suit?
The OIT building was not only open to the guests of the NFÜ, but also to the Ministry of Judiciary and Polices, which held the Lucia Ball there instead of a Christmas party on 7 December.
They did not have to pay rent to OIT for the use of the premises.
About 500 visitors came to the ball.
The festivities were opened with a speech by the minister, after the dinner there was live music for dancing.
At the request of [origo] it was announced: There was no ball goal or suit obligation on the ball.
The Ministries of Health, Labour, and Self-Governanment are each preparing for puritanical celebrations.
The Ministry of Health organizes the celebration for the employees in their own four walls for around 400,000 forints.
Labor Minister Mónika Lamperth officially invites her employees not at all to a Christmas party, but for the conference at the end of the year.
One expects with about 180 guests, who - according to the press department - are only entertained with coffee and pogates, so the celebration goes out with 350,000 forints.
Self-government Minister Gordon Bajnai and his colleagues will simply toast in the auditorium of the ministry.
The Ministry of Finances (PM) organizes a Christmas party in its own ballroom, to which 436 employees are expected.
The expenses were measured with 2,500 forints per capita - there is a buffet reception for this money, followed by toasting.
Since the beginning of J�ános Veres's tenure, PM employees have been celebrating this way every year.
However, there will be no real party atmosphere - not only because of the minister's official speech, but also because the celebration was scheduled for the afternoon.
The Ministry of Agriculture invites you to a New Year's reception on January 20, this time the Christmas party of the ministry fails.
You ask the one hundred guests at Vajdahunyad Castle in the Stadtpark ("Városliget"); according to information from the department, the expenses are measured with 1,000 forints per guest.
As the press department reports, it becomes a very simple standing reception, to which foreign diplomats accredited only in Hungary are invited.
The receptions held every year traditionally begin with a brief address from the minister and end with restrained lurking.
As the corresponding press offices report at [origo] request, there will not be a Christmas party at the Ministry of Foreign Affairs or the Ministry of Education.
Asked about the cost of the Christmas party you could not give information to the press department of the Ministry of the Environment.
In any case, more than 400 guests were invited to the celebration, which is organized in the Kunstgewerbemuseum.
More and more forces mobilized for giant strike
The League of Unions and the Hungarian Trade Union Federation are forming their coordination committee on Friday for Monday's strike indefinite duration.
On Saturday, a protesting-greater assembly will be helded, on Monday - on the day of the referendum on the health insurance law - the work stoppage begins.
Strike-related, significant traffic obstructions are expected, as rail traffic is almost completely discontinued, and the Budapest public transport companies (BKV) also strike until 13 o'clock.
Details of Monday's strike will be jointly announced on Friday afternoon by the Unions League and the trade union federation.
There will also be a LIGA - MOSZ strike committee responsible for coordinating Monday's actions.
On Saturday, a large-scale protest meeting will be held, and as of Monday, all organisations that have joined the strike will put down work indefinitely unless an agreement is reached with the government.
In other words, the end of the strike, which began on the day of parliamentary vote on the health insurance law, was not defined.
If the National Council fails to adopt the health insurance law, the unions will immediately withdraw the call for a strike.
However, if the law is passed, the strike committees will meet to discuss how to proceed.
Due to the agenda of the National Council on Monday, it is even conceivable that there will only be a vote on the health insurance law in the later evening hours, or even at midnight.
Due to the strikes starting on Monday, one will have to expect significant traffic obstructions: The railway workers put down their work indefinitely, in the morning the majority of public transport and more than half of the Volán societies stand still.
At the same time, there will be highway closures in many places.
Numerous trade unions and civilian organisations have followed the call for a strike - the list of participants is constantly updated on the league website.
Some organizations announced strikes indefinitely, others are planning a temporary work stoppage.
Until Thursday afternoon, an agreement has not yet been reached between the Hungarian Railway MÁV and the Free Trade Union of Railway Workers VDSZSZ, even though more and more people are traveling by train, especially around the Christmas holidays.
According to previous reports, the BKV is on strike from the start of operations until 1 p.m., between 7 a.m. and 9 a.m. at Budapest Airport, the LIGA trade unions for electricity and heat energy stop work for four hours, and the teachers’ union or the Democratic Teachers’ Union has called for strikes of 2 and 2-8 hours, respectively.
The Hungarian Medical Association strikes nationwide alternately at various times, the EDDSZ, the democratic union of employees in the health and social sector, a sub-organization of the SZEF, the forum for cooperation of the trade unions, holds a two-hour warning strike.
The most important of the four points demanded by the League is that the government withdraws the draft reform of health insurance and submits a new conception for social discourse.
The second demand concerns the gradual introduction of the new pension calculation model, which is due from next year.
Demand number three is that access to the early retirement system is already made possible for eligible occupational groups before 2010.
The fourth point is that VDSZSZ, which belongs to the League unions, has not accepted the offer made by the Ministry of Transport and is now calling for a new concept.
Polital Capital: Hungarian guard raises state risk
According to the Risk Analysing Division of Political Capital, the activities of the Hungarian Guard, which was founded this August with the support of the "Movement for a Better Hungary", have increased the risk of Hungary and the region in several respects.
Among other things, the institute's "Risk Warning Report" states: In connection with the activities of the Hungarian Guard, the highest political risk represents the "aggravation of the pre-existing ethnic tensions between the Gypsies and non-gypsy population in Hungary, which could even lead to violent ethnic conflicts in the short term".
The main reason for this is the strategy of the Hungarian Guard, which presumably aims at the provocation of such a dispute, according to the research institute.
As reported, this is seen as a failure on the part of the political leaders: "Persons of public life have left the discussion of the Roma issue to representatives of radical political groups who have now based their political strategy on the Roma-Hungarian opposition".
It is suspected that this would exacerbate the problem, make reasonable dialogue impossible, favor extremities, as well as increase the likelihood of violent confrontations.
The research institute also thinks that, not least thanks to the enormous attention on the part of the domestic and foreign media, the activities of the Hungarian Guard have "significantly harmed Hungary's image abroad, in particular with regard to confidence in stability and democratic maturity".
We remember: Since autumn 2006, the reorganization of radical and far-right groups in Hungary has been continuously upswing, so that since the summer of 2007, several "national self-defense associations" have been founded.
"It is common to these groups that they each define themselves as a civilian organization, while openly bragging about preparing their members for - sometimes armed - defensive tasks (disaster protection, national defense, order protection), citing the unsuitability of the police, the army, or the state itself," it said.
According to the method, all this is done, which seeks to disguise various political aspirations as a bourgeois initiative based on the political and party disenchantment of the population.
The emphasis on the civilian character and the form of the association thus serve not only to circumvent legal hurdles, which could arise in the course of registration in court - it is also a political strategy.
According to the analysment, the activities of the Hungarian Guard represent a risk-increasing factor for relations with neighbouring countries.
"In recent months the Hungarian Guard has been cited in several neighbouring states as a potential source of hazard, according to some ultra-nationalist politicians of certain neighbouring countries see in the emergence of the Hungarian Guard the confirmation of its own anti-Hungary policy.
All of this has an impact on the region's political stability," it said.
From the point of view of the research institute, the founding of the Hungarian Guard is not an individual phenomenon in Central-Eastern Europe, so the political risk for the entire region has grown.
As reported, the activities of the Hungarian Guard are comparable to those of the Bulgarian National Guard, whose foundation was announced in August 2007 by the far-right Bulgarian National Union (BNU).
In the background of the formation of the Bulgarian Guard were the Roma mass riots, which took place in a neighborhood of Sofia (Krasna Poljana).
The BNU has taken the incidents as an opportunity to establish the National Guard, arguing that the aim of "national protection".
In their opinion, a veritable "gypsy terrorism" was noticeable in Bulgaria in the past 17 years, but the state remained idle and indifferent - according to Political Capital.
And further: The initiative would have kicked off a fierce debate in the Bulgarian public.
As I said, the increased risk of ethnic clashes is also evidenced by the fact that some Bulgarian Roma organizations have announced that they are establishing their own guard for resistance purposes.
Since the activities of the Hungarian Guard so far show many similarities with those of the Bulgarian National Guard, it cannot be ruled out that the Hungarian Roma is also responding to the threats by organising its own protection, according to Political Capital’s risk warning report.
Attila Juhász, research director at Political Capital, said at the request of the news office: Risk analysis is one of the activities of the research institute.
Every year, risk analysis reports would be published, and should risk-changing tendencies be recognizable, public opinion will be informed, as in the case by issuing the risk warning report.
Festivities aren't holidays everywhere
In Hungary, public holidays are precisely regulated by law, so that the practice of rescheduling the working days around the holidays has now established itself, but the coming year will give us fewer such days.
Also in the EU and in America one understands something else by "holiday".
The Hungarian legal system distinguishes between state holidays and non-working days.
Work-free days are mostly ecclesiastical holidays, or are related to significant historical events.
For example, we also celebrate New Year's Day: Both the last day of the old and the first day of the new year are work-free days - the latter alone so that the New Year's Eve recreators can rest.
Throughout the year we have a total of fourteen holidays.
The state holidays are March 15 commemorating the revolution of 1848, and October 23, the commemoration day of the revolution of 1956.
On August 20, the founding of the state is celebrated, on May 1, on November 1 and 2, we commemorate our dead.
In addition, there are two holidays for Easter, Pentecost, and Christmas.
Since our state, national or religious holidays rarely fall on a weekend, the practice of "working day postponement" has proven itself in cases where we would only work one day before or after the holiday.
However, the price of extended weekends is that we have to work very well on the Saturdays before, or go to school.
When this day, which falls before or after the holiday, should ultimately be processed, has been stipulated for many years by the Minister of Labour in its own regulation.
The legislation applies to all employees and employees, and civil servants alike.
Excluded are only companies and institutions in which the operation is continued without interruption, i.e. also on the holidays.
This year, the working regulations will only be somewhat confused by the holidays at the end of the year: From 24 December, life in this country will literally stop, since most take time equalization for the days between Christmas and New Year's Eve, or take the specially cancelled holiday.
Next year there will be more holidays in the middle of the week, or to the end of the week, so there will be fewer reschedules during the working days than this year.
The calendar is "turned upside down" first around Labor Day so that people can stay at home for four days at a time.
Saturday, April 26 will be a working day so that Friday, May 2 can be a rest day.
The second rescheduling is related to the holiday on 23 October: on Saturday, 18 October you will have to work off Friday, 24 October in advance.
The end of the year, however, will be completely marked by the holidays, after from December 24 everything will lie and stop, even if New Year's Eve falls on a Wednesday.
Six chilling trifles to annoy work colleagues
E-mails that we forget to send, voice messages that we never receive - harmless lies that we no longer notice: they bring "color" into everyday life in the workplace.
The only question is whether you get used to these small inconveniences, or if you better rise to the challenge and try to consciously avoid these misunderstandable, for other annoying situations.
tips.
"Sorry, I forgot to tell you that someone called from the marketing department" - says a colleague.
That you missed a meeting?
At bestime, one regrets it and tries to make up for it by perhaps calling the marketing department and confessing remorsefully that one had forgotten to align the message (that is the less usual method).
Most of the time is dismissed with an apology.
Before getting involved in embarrassing excuses, you should think about how you can really give your colleagues unpleasant moments and try to consciously avoid these situations.
Borrowed something that doesn’t belong to you?
There’s hardly anything more annoying than if you’re reaching for a document, office supplies, or the phone, but it’s not there.
Yesterday, the stapliner was still lying on the desk - this morning it's gone?
Or is the lost ball pen discovered on a colleague's desk?
Without asking, one should not borrow anything from the colleagues, because they are rightened if they find that something has come away.
Well, don't put envelopes or post-it in your pocket from the office, because it could hit you yourself: no one left, if you desperately need it.
Gossip about colleagues' private lives?
There is no effective means of stopping a gossip that has already been put into the world.
Who with whom, what done?
where and why?
The information (or presumptions) about the private life of the colleagues should not bind to everyone's nose if possible!
And if someone opens up and betrays us his innermost secrecy, we should not abuse this trust!
It is best to remain silent like a tomb, instead of aligning those who are not present, even if others also know about the problems of the colleague.
Reported sick for no reason?
If you call in the office in order to report yourself sick, citing an excruciating headache, because you feel uncomfortable right now, or just want to rest, you should be aware of one thing: your own work, indefensible executions, projects with fixed appointments must now be done by the colleagues instead of yourself.
So don't nake going with colleagues!
If you already know the day before that you will not make it to the office the next day, or that something could come up, which is why you have to stay at home, you should report it in time so that neither the colleagues nor the boss are unexpectedly confronted with the absence.
Buttles, giggles, phone calls
One is just engrossed in his work, but is suddenly disturbed by the loud giker of the colleagues?
Unintentionally listening to a phone conversation you shouldn't have heared?
You shouldn't annoy your colleagues with buzzes, singing, loud conversations (which you lead over their heads), tramples, constant scolding or nagging.
All this can be very disruptive, even if they aren’t exactly engrossed in a task (writing, planning, organizing).
Aren't the others always to blame?
If a project is not finished on time, you have forgotten to send an important letter or important messages, you should not look for the fault of your own forgetfulness with others!
If possible, you shouldn't talk about the chaos in the office or the noisy colleagues!
It’s best to admit your mistakes, and apologize if others were affected!
The question of how to make up for the mistake, or to make up for what has been missed, is all too appropriate!
In the office - a feeling like at home?
Trackpants and slots: Convenient at home, but hasn’t lost anything in the office!
You shouldn’t even dress so casually, if the workplace would actually allow it!
Don't get too sloppy - you should respect both your colleagues and yourself to such an extent that you dress in the office - if desired, also pretty and elegant!
Want to do it right for everyone - or how do you become uninteresting for others
Stop and go traffic, the car in the middle of everyday traffic jams.
A strange melody resounds from the radio: the sound of a violin, paired with electronic effects.
I'll turn the volume higher.
The tempo changes, to the previous sounds comes the bass, and as a real song jewel a female choir.
It's Zagar's song "Wings of Love," resounded on mr2.
The local music scene hasn’t spoiled us for a long time with qualitative electropop.
But now you can even see the traffic jam differently when you listen to Zagar’s new album.
The appearance of Yonderboi, so many new and exciting production from the domestic digital underground scene slowly contribute to a change in the overall picture, and even if the garage rock with its guitar squeal is "in" again, which proves domestic electropop: there is something new under the sun.
Balázs Zságer started in Pécs, made music even in the band Yonderboi before - perhaps because a tavern cannot tolerate two pipers - began to tread his own path.
However, the breakthrough with his project (Zagar) and his music, the elements of jazz, electronic, and pop merged, only when one had approached him with the request to compose the soundtrack to a film (e.g.
"Szezon" by Ferenc Török).
The formation, which had initially appeared in smaller clubs, only became known to the wider audience when the magazine "wan2" lined up its first album from 2002 among the top 50 domestic albums and Zagar, together with Yonderboi, was the almost only trendsetter, also marketable in the West.
Undoubtedly Zagar and his band were "trendy," but it didn't interest anyone if they liked to wear this brand, too.
The band has reacted as it should: with a new, even better album.
The new new album entitled "Cannot Walk Fly Instead" could open up new ways for them, but the title already expresses the desire for continuity with the Pécs years.
The expression comes from the time when Balázs Zságer was still traveling with another band.
The airy, flying allusion would also be fine, but in addition to the ethereal character of the music, the - corresponding to the zeitgeist and the trends - louder guitar, the influences of rock 'n' roll.
György Ligeti, who lives in England and works with the band "The Puzzle", introduced himself at the premiere in Millenáris.
A song from the album has even made it to the radio hit - mr2 Petőfi, who has recently sent pop music from early to late, has successivelyly set the earworm to the listeners with the number "Wings of Love", which was recorded with the participation of six local singers ("Underground Divas").
All right so (?).
The hvg.hu asked Balázs Zságer.
hvg.hu: This new hit raises the question: To what extent is your music reserved for a certain layer?
Balázs Zságer: The term "shift music" doesn't exactly mean much to me.
In my opinion, there are two boxes in pop music, not mainstream and underground, or alternative and pop, but commercial and honest.
Even the so-called alternative or underground music can be shallow and meaningless, but they can also only be produced for the sale, which makes them commercial music.
Even a good pop number can arise from an honest motif.
The listener’s readiness also functions according to this principle, regardless of languages and national borders.
hvg.hu: Do you see a difference in the openness of the Hungarian and the foreign audience towards your music?
B. Zs.: Abroad, especially west of us, pop music has a greater tradition.
Not only teenagers or twens go to concerts, but also the older audience.
They follow the trends in pop music happening around them much more observantly because they also consider them a part of contemporary culture.
hvg.hu: Which role does the audience play in your work?
Is it important, who, where, how many listen to your music?
B. Zs.: Of course it’s a good feeling to see that more and more come to our concerts.
However, we have never composed for the market.
With us, the music does not arise to serve a purpose, but for a specific reason.
The role of the audience is mostly noticeable at the concerts, where there is already a mutual interaction, but this in turn exists only in the intangible changes of the moment.
Nevertheless, I have noticed one thing: If you want to please everyone, you are no longer interesting.
hvg.hu: How far do you want to follow Yonderboi?
He's on the road as a soloist and's been more likely to build his career in the West
We don’t really go our way, apart from the fact that you keep trying to interpret this into our music.
We see ourselves more as team players who rely on each other’s musical presence and let them unfold.
Initially, of course, comparisons of our work were inevitable with that of Yonderboi, as several of us came out of his band and took a different path.
hvg.hu: In the charts of mr2 Petőfi "Wings of Love" comes first.
Many have only heard the name Zagar in connection with this number.
Was the opening deliberately staged with a slightly more popular style towards a wider audience?
We didn’t have any plans to write a hit, which is supposed to serve as a draught horse for the album.
This song is about more.
It was intended as the finale of the album, with which we wanted to dissolve the sound orgy and the slightly more difficult themes.
hvg.hu: How did you choose the six singers?
Why exactly her, what was the guiding principle?
B. Zs.: The basic idea was to make a gospel-like choir.
Then came the idea to put together the choir of talented soloists from the local music scene.
That's how the six-member choir originated - that's Sena, Edina Kutzora, Judie Jay, Bori Péterfy, Enikő Hodosi [Neo], Juci Németh - whom we then baptized "Underground Divas".
hvg.hu: Did you want to have the song the same way, or did the girls co-create it?
B. Zs.: The number has been in the instrumental version for a long time, but when the material for the album was finished, we thought that this number still "needs something".
The chord sequel has formally demanded a singing voice.
I sung the melody that same night, later they learned the girls.
It all came practically immediately and terrifically spontaneous.
I can remember I was quite electrified at the time, but the "Divas" already needed it.
With my voice alone, the song would never have been so powerful.
hvg.hu: However, both on the record and at the concert had the impression that the girls were in the background.
There's no solo part (apart from Judy Jay's short solo), they don't have a role of their own, singing completely together and aren't louder than the music, so their vocals aren't just accompanied by the music.
Also on the stage they stood in the back.
Is it intentional?
B.Zs.: The fact that they stood in the back was so because of purely technical problems.
However, we deliberately didn’t want to give them solo roles.
The number is also about forgetting your ego a little, forgetting who came from where and where is going, let's just allow these certain wings, the wings of love to lift us.
I'd like others to feel the euphoric state we felt when the song originated.
hvg.hu: Which was the most elaborate song?
B. Zs.: In terms of musical work, there have actually been none at all, there have been more difficult tasks when mixing.
For example in "Dorian Gray's Dilemma", in which I play on a concert piano, or "Rock'n'Roll Goes To The Girls", where the groove did not want to work for a long time, but little by little everything has merged up, as with a puzzle.
hvg.hu: Which is your favorite number from the new album?
B. Zs.: That changes constantly with the concerts and even now, where I "let go" of the album.
"Prophet Is A Fool", "Escape From The Earth" and "Never The Same".
Fraudulent broker risked billions because of feelings of inferiority
Bottomless scammer
This is how his former employee Jerome Kerviel describes the French banking giant Société Générale, owner of the local Komerční banka.
Because of him, it came by almost five billion euros.
But it seems that Kerviel was only a great player who wanted to do the same to his colleagues, was certainly not incapable.
In contrast to all the major banks, he recognized the crisis in the American mortgage market in time.
"He's mentally rather weak," was heard from the mouth of his superiors, who reported him to the police.
But according to Kerviel’s statements published by the French media, for example the daily Le Monde, he was a person who had made profits of 1.6 billion euros with his dealings at the end of last year.
Crisis not only in America
"Anything I wanted was to make money for my bank," he claims in his statement.
In the summer, he had managed to earn half a billion euros just on the American crisis.
He had already spied the opportunity at the beginning of the year.
In February, there was already initial news that so-called sub-prime pharmacies, such as low-income customers or those who had already had payment problems before, would get into trouble.
However, this crisis should not have an impact on the entire economy.
Kerviel didn't believe that and was right.
At the end of the year, he had increased his profit up to 1.6 billion euros.
"Even when I'd earned the first half a billion, I didn't know how to explain it," Kerviel admits and goes on to say he definitely didn't keep anything for himself.
During all the interrogation, he admits that he faked documents so that he would not be caught.
He even managed to deceive his bosses, even as the Eurex stock market gradually became interested in his businesses.
Risk Profit
Did he fear being exposed?
It's hardly.
"I thought she satisfied the simple fact that I made 1.6 billion for the bank."
Where did he get the courage to risk so much?
He had to develop his position gradually.
He joined Société Générale in 2000, immediately after completing his studies at Lyon University.
His annual salary amounted to about 51000 dollars.
After two years he was already an assistant to a broker and in 2005 he became a self-employed broker with an annual salary of 90000 dollars.
Still, he knew he'll never earn as much as others.
"Already during my first conversation in 2005, I knew I was being watched.
Because of my training and also because of my professional and personal development," Kerviel explained to the investigating officers.
But soon after his entry came the first great success.
He earned a lot from Allianz's shares by betting half a billion euros on price losses of this title in the London bombings.
And that was the trigger - now he wanted more.
As long as you're winning, we're gonna
"It's like a snowball," he told cops.
But the bosses allegedly knew about everything, or guessed.
"As long as I made money, you closed your eyes."
But then at the beginning of this year came the big blow.
In a single day, he lost one billion euros.
One weekend in mid-January, he wanted to make up for that
When he came to work on Monday, he was no longer an employee of the bank.
And a few days later, the bank proclaimed that it had lost five billion euros because of it.
And how did they come upon him?
Suspiciously, he went on holiday.
"I only took four days from the previous year.
A broker who doesn't take a vacation is one who doesn't want you to look him into the cards," Kerviel concluded.
The Czech Republic has the chance to get to 365 billion
Czech ministers and representatives of the European Commission signed a total of nine development programmes yesterday, through which the Czech Republic can reach 14 billion euros (about 365 billion crowns) over the next few years.
It accounts for about half of the sum that the Czech Republic can even receive from Brussels in the years 2007 to 2013.
About 125 billion crowns come from seven regional development programmes.
Czech firms will be able to be allocated about 100 billion crowns from the "Company and Innovation" program.
Most money, about 150 billion, will flow into traffic expansion.
The transport programme is the largest development programme and comprises up to 22 percent of the funds that the Czech Republic can receive from the fund.
This money will increase the budget of the State Transport Infrastructure Fund, which will have 90.8 billion next year.
The "Company and Innovation" program is intended to serve entrepreneurs and companies to attract funds to innovative ventures.
As a result, the companies are supposed to gain finance for the introduction of new technologies, for corporate research, but also for the introduction of more effective organizational methods.
In the previous money acquisition from EU funds, the Czech Republic is one of the least successful among the new candidate countries so far.
Nicaraguan court upholds twenty-year prison sentence for ex-president
An appeals court in Nicaragua on Thursday upheld the twenty-year sentence for former President Arnold Alemán, to which he had been sentenced in 2003 for financial fraud totaling 100 million dollars (nearly 1.8 billion kronor).
Later, however, he had been released from prison and lived under house arrest on his hazienda near the Nicaraguan metropolis.
Whether the former head of state (1997 to 2002) will get this Central American state behind bars is currently unclear, according to the AP news agency.
According to Ileana Pérez, the chairman of the appeals court, it will now depend on the detention officers on whether Alemán is allowed to serve his sentence at home, or whether he has to go to jail.
A court four years ago showed that Alemán had regularly shifted money from the government to accounts of foreign firms in Panama.
These companies were fictitious and the accounts actually belonged to his relatives and closest followers.
Some of this money eventually ended up in a fund that Alemán had set up to finance his 2001 election campaign.
Budget's through
Yeah, it's done.
The state budget is approved.
What's special about this budget?
It is the first budget this government has drafted, and which has been adopted without the support of the opposition by the coalition majority.
He was still approved for the current year with the support of the ČSSD (Social Democrats) because Minister Sobotka (from this party) had presented him, and the ČSSD was therefore unable to reject him.
In contrast to the lively debate before the vote on the motion of no confidence against the government, nothing dramatic happened this time.
The House of Representatives quietly endorsed this law, which has traditionally paid little attention, but is highly significant.
That's kind of paradoxical.
All governing begins and ends with the individual chapters and items of the household, whether you like this or not.
The budget is an integral part of politics, but is obscured by the power struggle of the government and the opposition.
This is a more grateful topic for the media, and that's why they pay more attention to it.
A quarrel among politicians is much more interesting to the public than sober numbers.
State budgets suffered from three weak points among the past social democratic governments that the 2008 budget cannot eliminate at once:
The first is the high proportion of spending, which is mandatory by law, which is not only about pensions, but also social spending that the "tacitive" coalition of ČSSD and KSČM (communists) decided last year before the elections.
The second is deficient fiscal policy as a result of the first weak.
The third limitation is the deficits of public finances, i.e. the budget deficits of the municipalities.
However, the biggest risk isn't the government budget deficit.
This budget is public and reasonably under control.
However, the budget deficit of the municipalities is a true time bomb.
Many mayors, in addition to income from taxes, levies and bank lenders, often issued other sources for investments, often unconsidered bonds, through which they indebted their town halls in excess.
In recent years, they have managed to pay off this debt by massively selling shares of the energy and other supply networks they had received from the state in the course of privatization.
Now this source has already dried up.
As far as we adhere only to official and verifiable facts, we find that:
Our economy, like the rest of the world, has been going through a period of growth for several years, of which the ČSSD boasts without ceasing.
This represented a once-in-a-lifeth opportunity for their governments to put public finances in order.
Instead, however, these governments indebted the state even more.
Other countries, apart from a few exceptions such as Hungary, Albania, Pakistan or Egypt, have managed much more reasonably than our republic.
This is a huge shame, mainly due to the previous governments.
We are among the countries that, seen on a global scale, are least at risk of poverty.
Therefore, no massive social programs are needed, which have unfortunately been influcted to a gigantic extent, especially in recent years.
Even though it has been possible to eliminate some of the nonsensical populist spending passed this year before the elections, it is clear that in the near future there will not be a postponement of the retirement age and pension reform, then virtually any politics in this country ends, and, no matter those who is in power, it will have nothing but pure management.
It's only slightly exaggerated when you say that ruling politicians then become civil servants that mechanically divide most of the tax revenue into pensions and social spending.
Therefore, this year's budget cannot be a revolution, but can only put a stop to the suppression of normal expenditure through compulsory spending.
The deficit of 70 billion kronor envisaged for 2008 would be superfluous if previous governments hadn't spent over the past decade, which only went.
If we do not want it really to happen that ruling politicians become purely administrative officials, then we must be patient and this not too strong coalition government, which, however, is aware that the state can only spend as much as it is able to take taxes and fees, in the preparation of further reform laws.
Only these make it possible to progressively enforce a balanced state budget.
Jiří Krejčí
Banks want to manage crisis
The U.S. Federal Reserve, the European Central Bank and the central banks of Britain, Switzerland and Canada have decided to fight together against the crisis in the financial markets.
Thus, more than 100 billion dollars by means of auctions will reach the currency markets.
At these auctions, banks are auctioning interest rates at which they are willing to lend money to each other.
For example, the American Fed sends 40 billion dollars at auction, with another 24 making it loose for the European and Swiss central banks.
It is the first time after the attacks of 9/11 that the central banks of the individual states agree on such a coordinated approach to prevent or mitigate the consequences of a financial crisis.
"This move is intended to eliminate the greatest difficulties of the economy, i.e. the increasing lack of freely available capital that the banks can lend," wrote in a report for its clients Bernard Baumohl of consulting firm Economic Outlook Group.
"Coordinated action by central banks has reduced the likelihood of a recession in the U.S. from 65% to about 50%," Baumohl added in his report.
Banks have already lost around 60 billion dollars because of unserved loans, especially in the United States.
Analysts expect further billions in losses.
"The central banks must have been really afraid of what else can happen when it decided on this course of action," ABN Amro's chief economist Kieran Davies said.
"It looks like the situation is really bad," he continued.
The central banks fear that if the banks themselves have difficult access to bonds, loans will also become more expensive for their customers.
That would reduce consumption and plunge the American economy into a recession, possibly with an impact on economic growth around the world.
The central banks have decided to do so after failing to lower the so-called LIBOR, the London interbank interest rate, which is used in money lending between banks.
This means that the banks were constantly afraid to borrow money from each other, even though central banks of the US, Canada and the UK had lowered their interest rates several times during the autumn.
"That will have the same effect as a further cut-off in interest rates," Greg Salvaggio of Tempus Consulting told Reuters.
Some analysts believe that this move by central banks contributes to heating up the crisis because it does not solve the fundamental problem, namely the fall of the U.S
Klaus: Ecologist debates are unbelievable
Global climate changes reportedly hurt developing countries the most.
President Václav Klaus again commented on the issue of global warming.
Criticism now referred to the U.N. international conference on climate change in Bali.
In Vienna, where he presented his book "A blue, not a green planet," he stated that ecologists' debates were unbelievable.
According to Klaus, not only is the structure of the conference unacceptable, which forms a strange mixture of government representatives and those of non-government organizations, but also the fundamental difference between the rhetoric it accompanies and reality.
"The debate there is unstructured, it's a word firework that's only a substitute for a real solution to current problems," Klaus said.
At the presentation of his book, the Czech President emphasized that his topic was very important to him, and that he was so committed to it.
He also expressed his satisfaction that his book appeared in German translation because "I often meet caricatures of my views."
So now interested parties could read his attitude unabashedly.
Klaus recalled that he was not a climatologist, but in his opinion he had sufficient prerequisites to write such a book.
After all, he is an academist, able to understand and assess professional treatises, has experience with statistics and is also an economist.
As the main message of the book, he described the thesis that the climate is constantly changing and that its changes are therefore normal, that there is no consistent opinion among the scientists that the consequences of the current changes are not too large and serious, and that efforts to influence the development of the climate are superfluous, ineffective and unacceptably complex.
In addition, as someone who has experience with totalitarian systems, he thinks it is unacceptable to impose unproven views on others, he sees this as a dangerous restriction of freedom.
In response to questions from attendees, he repeatedly declared that he was not opposed to "sensible environmental protection, but against militant eco-activists who have no regard for the relationship between effort and yield."
Klaus had already talked about the same topic in Vienna two months ago at the Hayek Institute.
600000 people failed to exchange their driver's license
600000 motorists missed the deadline to exchange their old driver's license, the validity of which expires at the end of the year.
Today is the last day where you can apply for the exchange so that the authorities manage to issue a new driver's license by the end of the year.
However, the ministry does not fear that after New Year, hundreds of thousands of drivers will take to the roads without a valid driving license.
According to the authorities, mainly those who do not want to drive have exchanged their driver's license.
While small places report a sharp drop in interest in leader's ticket exchanges, the capital's offices are showered with applications, and interest in exchanging grew this week.
The city administration handles around 2000 customers every day.
You'll have to queue for about half an hour
In Prague, about 140000 motorists haven't exchanged their driver's license yet.
The issuance of a new driver's license lasts 20 days.
But it is possible to apply for the issuance with a shortened deadline of five days.
In this case, however, the driver will have to add 500 crowns.
Motorists who do not make the prescribed exchange by the end of the year and leave without a valid driver's license are threatened with a fine from January.
The culprit pays a maximum of 2000 crowns.
However, the police can also use an admonition during the transgression.
In the event of transgression, the penalty may rise to 2500 crowns.
According to previous information, even those of the municipality who does not drive at all can be punished by the municipality.
According to him, he could face a penalty of 30000 crowns.
The ministry already called on the offices to exercise clemency and impose at most a penalty of 1000 crowns.
According to the spokesperson of the Ministry of Transport, Karel Hanzelka, however, does not threaten anyone with a thousand-crown penalty.
The driver's license is a document in which everyone can decide whether he wants to own it or not.
It is not an identity card that everyone, namely a valid one, must have.
People who don't want to drive anymore should drop off their driver's license.
It is sufficient to take him to the responsible municipal office or send him there by registered mail.
At the end of the year, driving licences issued between 1964 and 1993 become invalid.
However, motorists have to gradually exchange other types of driving licenses.
People with driving licenses from 1994 to 2000 still have three years to exchange.
The validity of driver's licenses issued from 2001 to 2004 expires in December 2013.
According to Hanzelka, there are about 2.6 million more driving licenses.
Court lowers penalties for detectives allied to Berdych
Former policians Josef Opava and Petr Koňařík, who are accused of making a common cause with David Berdych's gang, received 13 and 10 years imprisonment respectively.
This was decided in a final judgment by the Prague Higher Regional Court, which reduced its sentence by one or two years.
Originally, the detectives of the "Office for the Fight Against Organized Crime" threatened 14 and 12 years, respectively.
There is no possibility of appeal against today's judgment, only revision at the Supreme Court is possible.
Berdych himself is also charged in this matter, but he is initially faced with the first-instance verdict.
At other trials, however, he had already received sentences totaling 16 years.
He cannot be condemned for anything more.
In January, a district tribunal pleaded guilty to 19 people mainly for involvement in robberies, the Higher Regional Court today negotiated the appeal of 16 of them.
Most of the defendants denied the indictment from the outset on the grounds that it was a constructed case.
According to the Central Bohemian district court, members of the police handed over information from police sources in consultation with Berdych criminals, covered various robberies and helped members of the gang evade prosecution.
The gang left Opava and Koňařík 30 percent of the loot.
The indictment deals with crimes committed by the gang from 1999 to 2001.
In particular, robberies, whose victims often suffered serious injuries, but also for presumption of office and illicit possession of weapons.
During house searches, the police discovered illegal pistols, ammunition with a steel core, a laser riflescope and a submachine gun.
The gang, according to the indictment, has enriched itself by many millions of crowns through their robberies.
In addition to almost 20 men in these proceedings, courts recognized Berdych and a number of others for further robberies as legally guilty.
More men from Berdych's circle have to answer in another trial, further cases have not yet come to court.
Tomáš Půta and Maroš Šulej, who are imprisoned Irish and are awaiting the final decision on their expatriation for prosecution in the Czech Republic, have so far escaped the main hearing.
Children swapped - bill: 12 million
For the exchange of children in a maternity hospital, the lawyer of the aggrieved parents, Igor Veleba, wants to demand from the hospital in Třebíč 12 million-kron compensation plus court costs.
For each of the mothers three million, for the fathers two million and for the children one million each.
The hospital offers a maximum of 200000 for each parental couple.
The legal representative of the hospital, Ondřej Dostál, is therefore not at all agreement with Veleba's proposal.
"We reject such a proposal, it is many times too high," Dostál said after today's trial.
"However, in order to be able to form an opinion on this counter-proposal, we need the expert opinion, which the other side had prepared.
We'll see if it's even meaningful.
However, we insist on the view that this should be in the hundreds of thousands for everyone together," Dostál stressed.
According to him, the amount should move between 100000 and 200000 crowns for each of the four parents.
In the beginning, the family from Jabloňov wanted a million crowns for every month she had not raised her biological daughter.
Veleba served as the basis for compensation negotiations was an expert opinion prepared for him by the University Hospital in Prague Motol.
According to experts in psychology and psychiatry, the parents of the children suffered psychological damage and stress symptoms in varying strength and depth.
The men tolerated the stress situation better, even if they faced accusations that they could have prevented the confusion of the newborns.
The women, in turn, cope worse with the after-effects of experienced trauma, as well as with what is happening right now, Veleba summarized the contents.
Veronika and Nikola exchanged nurses at Třebíč on December 9 last year.
Last week, the children returned to their biological parents - Nikola to the Čermáks in Přibyslavice, and Veronika zu Libor Brož and Jaroslava Trojanová to Jabloňov.
At the end of October, the Vysočina (Bohemian-Moravian Height) district governor handed over 100000 crowns to each family as a replacement for expenses incurred by them due to the mistake of the hospital in Třebíč, which is subordinate to the district.
All hospitals in the Czech Republic are covered by law liability insurance.
In case of doubt, they have to resort to a reserve fund, only exceptionally they can apply for help from the institution.
The courts have so far awarded comparatively low amounts of damages to those affected, the amounts were determined by tables and moved in the range of hundreds of thousands, exceptionally millions.
Polívka came around his kingdom of Wallachia
Bolek Polívka lost the dispute over the "Kingdom of Wallachia" brand.
According to the judge at the district court in Ostrava, he was the first to use the designation "Wallach king", but the projection had built up a team led by Tomáš Harabiš in cooperation with the actor for many years of work.
Polívka did not attend the trial today.
According to his lawyer, however, the matter is probably not over and his client will probably appeal.
"The dispute is about Mr Harabiš and his team wanting to participate in the popularity of Bolek Polívka parasitically," Polívka's lawyer said after the trial.
At the words "Walachic Kingdom" everyone introduced themselves precisely to these actors and comedians.
Harabiš is also of the same opinion.
"We've got almost ten years of work behind us.
raising money and concepts of activities," he said.
According to him, the occasion is a dispute over money.
"After years, they have noticed that the project is successful and that they could also have some of it.
They want to gain parasitary work," he went on to say.
Lucrative Business
According to the court, Polívka used the designation "Walachian King" for the first time in one of his television broadcasts.
In 1993 he was crowned by Radoslav Brzobohatý.
At that time, however, this was not connected with the intention of establishing a fictitious kingdom.
A few years later, Harabiš came up with the idea of impersonating Wallach passports.
From an originally fun action, a business and an entrepreneurial activity crystallized, to which Harabiš then joined the foundation of the company "Wallachian Kingdom".
In 1997, Polívka agreed to the use of his name and his signature in the passports and even offered their owners discounts when visiting his farm and theatrical performances.
Who will sit on the throne?
The judiciary also stated today that Harabiš only used the mere idea that Polívka had made public in the show "Manege" for his entrepreneurial intentions.
"Polívka's popularity certainly helped the project of the Wallachian kingdom," the judge said.
However, she added that the soul of the Harabiš project and his team were deeply dedicated to him.
The cooperation of the parties, which are now divided, lasted until 2000, when Harabiš organized the official coronation for Polívka in Vsetín.
Since then, however, the disputes between Polívka and Harabiš began to accumulate, which culminated in the deposition "King Boleslav's I elected until Saint-Nimmerleins Day".
At Harabišs Jarmila Šuláková, the leadership role was used as Queen Mother.
In 2002, Polívka filed a lawsuit against Harabiš and his company, which the court dismissed today.
Clarity in the dispute could bring the Harabiš-prepared competition for the throne and half a princess.
A new king should emerge from this.
He did not want to reveal details and an appointment yet.
"We don't want to rush anything.
It was supposed to be something like the royal parade in Wallachia.
The king should be elected for a certain period of time.
Mr Polívka, too, can register quietlynessly," Harabiš added.
Test Peugeot 207 SW 1.6 VTi: advantages dwindling
To tell the truth, I've never been a special fan of the Peugeot 206.
His driving characteristics were not worth much, the quality of the gear shift, the steering and also the workmanship could be described as rather below-average.
But now his successor, the Peugeot 207, is on the market, and it is much better.
The combination with the designation SW is still a bit further, and I only have to revise my opinion.
When I had tested the classic hatchback of the 207, I had to complain, for example, about the insufficient headroom in the back seats.
These complaints no longer exist, because the body of the station wagon is designed in such a way that not even two-meter men will suffer.
Fast on
I'm only going through the interior for a short time, so as not to repeat myself too much.
It is a rather successful design, where I have objections only because of the meager central tunnel.
I would welcome a little more designer design, where the car otherwise makes such an elegant impression.
The seats are comfortable, and past are the days when we may complain about too short seats or headrests.
The side support has also improved considerably compared to 206.
As far as the range of rooms is concerned, you can also feel the increased dimensions of the body.
In combination, the aforementioned lack of head clearance is also eliminated.
Suitcase in the trunk
However, the main thing about this car is the cargo area.
I have to describe this (also because of the folding rear window) as very loadable and space-saving.
The cavities are equipped with holding belts, which are an ideal compromise.
They don't bother, get pulled apart and also hold heavier items.
But let’s talk about loading volumes.
According to the table, it amounts to 428 liters in the basic equipment, which is a rather average value compared to the competition.
However, not compared to Škoda Fabia Combi, the best-selling vehicle in the Czech Republic.
If we compare the 207 SW with the new generation of Mladá Boleslav product, we find that the Peugeot is left behind by a clearly noticeable 52 liters.
This value is already critical for a foreign holiday or when inviting a stroller.
But in order to highlight the Positiva, I still have to mention the very shallow cargo space or the very low loading edge after folding the rear seats.
120 hp in four rows
Our test car was equipped with a four-cylinder petrol engine with a displacement of 1.6 liters and an output of 88 kWp.
And honestly, that's what a 1600-cubic-engine should look like that's driving a family hatchback.
He's quiet, has low consumption and pulls away well.
If I compare him with his counterpart at Škoda, who sometimes acts as if his exhaust was clogged with a rag, then I can only be enthusiastic about him.
The price list speaks volumes
The price of this car isn't very popular.
However, we have to remember that we tested a well-equipped car with a spacious interior and a very good engine.
The basic price of the car with the 1400 petrol engine in the amount of 329900 krons is still on.
With our engine, it costs from 410900 crowns, which is already quite a lot.
The premium equipment of the test car strikes again with 80000 crowns in addition to beech.
However, we had head airbags, automatic air conditioning, automatic windshield wiper triggering, electric windows at the rear and...
So the car costs a total of simple 531900 crowns.
That’s a lot, but calculate the additional equipment of a Fabia and you will arrive at a very similar sum.
So there is only the decision of which brand you want to choose.
Integration of Slovak pupils in British schools does not succeed
At British schools, the number of children from countries in Eastern Europe is increasing, which raises fears among the locals about the further development of the local school system.
The integration of Eastern Europeans has so far encountered a number of obstacles.
British schools even detect cases where disputes between immigrants and the children of the original residents increase in violence.
The problem with the integration of children from Eastern Europe into British schools aptly illustrates the fall of North Kent, where clashes between locals and Slovak immigrants are gradually increasing.
Quarrels, harassment and scrambling among pupils have transferred to relations between parents.
The situation has already developed to such an extent that the police in Chatham arrested a Slovak woman who struck a ten-year-old boy with an iron bar.
The accused defended himself by saying that the boy had insulted her with racist insults.
Police also had to intervene on a Slovak immigrant after an attack by two underage girls.
The girls had allegedly made racist remarks to the man and pelted him with stones.
"When we went home, a boy climbed onto a bush house and spitted at us.
When I told him to stop, he threw apples and pears at us and then exposed his buttocks," one mother of Slovakian disciples told The Times newspaper described another awkward incident.
Complaints come from both sides.
One of the locals even described the dispute with the immigrants from Eastern Europe as a struggle for survival.
The cases cited testify that the mood between locals and immigrants at British schools is becoming increasingly tense.
In the UK, 70000 Slovaks are registered who came here looking for work.
About 30000 Slovak pupils attend British schools.
Cuba - most beautiful island in the Caribbean
For visitors, it really is an island of freedom.
Whatever you expect from your vacation, that will come true if you only choose the right thing.
Golden and white beaches, high waves or a sea that is quiet like a pond.
Palm trees and all-inclusive with waiters offering pizza, ice cream and beguiling cocktails on the beach.
Kitesurfing, golf and roulette.
On the islands of the Caribbean you will find this in excess.
However, you will only find much more on an island, and this island is Cuba, the largest and most most beautiful of the Greater Antilles.
At the time of the Spaniards, Cuba was their bastion for the conquest of America.
Columbus called it the most beautiful place that a human eye has ever seen.
Havanna once became a camp or transit camp of treasures that the Spanish had robbed in America and then transported to Europe.
And then came the American epoch.
Hotel Nacional was created at the very end of the 20th century.
One of the bosses of the American mafia, Lucky Luciano, was built, because everything that was considered a crime in the U.S. (particularly during Prohibition) was gladly seen and supported in Cuba - drugs, gambling, prostitution.
Among his guests were, among others.
Winston Churchill, Ava Gardner, Frank Sinatra.
High-rise buildings grew in Havana, across the wide boulevards drove cars that were among the cult symbols of the fifties of the last century.
Ernest Hemingway bought an estate in San Francisco de Paula, near Havanas, and in Cojímar he had his yacht.
On the walls of Bodeguita del Medio, stars of show business and politicians from all over America left their signatures.
And today?
On the walls of Havana Bodeguita you can also leave your autograph.
Cocktails can be mixed at the bar at Hotel Nacional as you wish.
And the cult cars of the Fifties drive around Havana to this day, because the once-imported Schigulis and Fiats in the salty and humid air have long since fallen apart.
Maybe Havana, in particular its oldest part, is heavily rumped, nowhere can you find so many wonderful colonial sights in the vicinity as just here.
Other places are being cleaned out right now, for example Trinidad.
In view of the fact that tourism brings much-needed money, there was the green light for this development.
In the last fifteen years, new hotels have not only been built in Havana, but also in Varadero, on the coast of Holguín province and also on islets such as Cayo Coco, Cayo Guillermo and others.
Cuba still offers a quirk that is unique in the world and especially attracts railway lovers.
The sugar cane from remote plantations brought vapour and diesel-powered narrow-gauge railways to the sugar factories.
Their fleet has not been renewed for decades, and so you can see cute and fully functioning locomotives there, whose peers have long since stood elsewhere in the museum.
Excursion destinations
The majority of Cuba's visitors do not miss the most important places.
So you don't have to write about Varadero or Havana in detail anymore.
It should only be noted that there is a museum in the already mentioned estate Hemingways, whose visit should not be missed by the one who likes this writer.
The Viñales valley in the west of the island is one of Cuba’s most picturesque corners.
Lush vegetation grows on lime soil.
The mogotas, limestone hills and limestones, protrude steeply from the bottom of the valley, in their cavies fertile soil accumulated, through which weathered ricker areas are covered by dense vegetation.
It’s this valley where perhaps the best Cuban tobacco is cultivated.
In its final form, you can meet him if you visit one of Havana's cigar factories.
It may also seem interesting to some people that the Chilean episode of the television series "Major Zeman" was filmed there.
Trinidad
An ancient city founded shortly after the Spaniards colonized the island.
Familiary and wealth brought her the trade of slaves and sugar.
Wealth embodied itself in the pretty beauty of the small town.
Spanish colonial architecture, heavily influenced by the later French settlers, has barely changed for four hundred years.
Trinidad, located between the sea and the Escambray mountains, is such a secluded place that there was still no road there in the fifties of the last century that would have connected the area to the rest of the country.
This insulation helped ensure it went unnoticed.
Nothing was built there, but that's why nothing was demolished, everyone was happy that he had a roof over his head.
If you have more than an afternoon for Trinidad, then it is worth looking at interesting places in the area, such as Playa Ancón beach or the "Valley of Sugar Factories".
In the evening you should go to the Casa de la Música, which enlives after darkness, and where the music is not silent until the morning.
Baracoa
This small town in eastern Cuba, some refer to it as the most beautiful on the island.
Surely this view is also influenced by the way there.
Both roads that lead there from Guantánamo have to overcome high and rugged mountains and run through a landscape that is hardly touched by human interventions.
Thanks to the untouched nature there, UNESCO included this eastern forepost of Cuba in its World Heritage directory.
There the best chocolate is made, in the church you show the cross of Columbus (in reality it was created about a hundred years after the journeys of Columbus), and from the hill where the former castle, the current Hotel El Castillo, stands, there are several table mountains, of which El Yunque is the most well-known.
About coffee, rum, cigars, Sierra Maestra mountains, Bay of Pines and Isla de Pinos Island, the Tropicana cabaret, the sanctuary of the Cuban patronist Caridad del Cobre, about the Santeria, an African religion to which the descendants of slaves profess, about all this one could write many pages.
However, as they say, look better with their own eyes.
Allies in Afghanistan are passive, Americans claim.
According to the United States, too few soldiers and the lack of necessary equipment influences the course of the mission in Afghanistan.
According to Defense Secretary Robert Gates, NATO should draft a concrete plan for the next few years.
The mission in Afghanistan does not proceed according to their ideas, about which the representatives of the countries who have stationed units in the south of the country agreed in Edinburgh, Scotland.
In addition to the growing resistance from the Taliban, the eight participating states are also discussing further coordination plans among allies.
As the United States, through its defense secretary Robert Gates, invoked NATO units for more activity, Britain sees priority in strengthening the authority of the Afghan government and in intensifying development aid.
"There is noticeable progress in Afghanistan, but military power can only be part of the solution.
Another part is to enable residents to start a further development of the economy and security themselves," British Defense Secretary Des Browne tells the BBC.
Ninety percent
Activity by Afghan insurgents has reached its highest level in five years, according to the American army.
The number of violent actions has risen by 27 percent compared to the previous year, in the southern province of Helmand even by sixty percent.
NATO countries have about 40000 troops stationed in Afghanistan, about a quarter of them in southern territory.
According to the United States, however, allies have reached an absolute limit in terms of units organization and equipment.
Specifically, they are calling for more helicopters, soldiers and experts to train the Afghan units.
"We're going to have to think earnestly abound the concrete goals for the next time and the way we can achieve them," Gates said.
NATO’s secretary-general, Jaap de Hoop, commented similarly today.
"I'm not pleased with what we've available in Afghanistan on land and in the air.
We've got about ninezy percent of the soldiers and equipment we're needing, and that's too little," De Hoop told the Reuters agency for three-day talks with the Japanese government in Tokyo.
Who's passive
Among the countries involved in recent months there has also been a dispute over whether all allies in Afghanistan are doing their best.
Because of passivity, Germany, for example, was criticized.
"The Germans are represented relatively numerously in Afghanistan.
But they are stationed in areas where there is only a minimum of violence and the fighting requirements for them are not as high as for us in the southern and eastern part of the country," Gates said.
The British tabloil newspaper "Sun" this week described the German soldiers as lazy.
"Afghanian non-dozziness unmasked: The Germans make games while our boys fight," wrote the "Sun" in a post supplemented by photos of German soldiers playing table tennis.
The German Bild newspaper went over to counter-attack and printed a picture of a British unit at a ruby competition in the southern part of Afghanistan.
Sovereign debt will soar to a trillion in 2009
Each Czech accounts for a national debt of 84000 crowns.
Total debt rose to 842.7 billion crowns by the end of September.
The government had planned to get into debt this year with 159.2 billion cones.
At the beginning of December, however, thanks to the better development of state fiscal policy, the ministry reduced the plan to 152.8 billion.
The Czech Republic's sovereign debt rose to 842.7 billion crowns by the end of September.
Within the first nine months of this year, they rose by 40.2 billions.
Since June, however, they fell by 13.4 billion.
The Ministry of Finance thus confirmed its information on the national debt from the beginning of October.
Each Czech has a debt of 84000 crowns.
Over the entire past year, the state was indebted by more than 111 billion crowns.
"Until now, it seems that, thanks to the lower deficit for the coming year, we may not exceed the limit of one trillion crowns until 2009.
Next year, the debt should range between 900 billion and a trillion crowns," David Marek, an analyst at Patria Finance, told server iHNed.cz.
Vladimír Pikora, an analyst at Next Finance, sees it similarly: "We could come to exceed one trillion in the course of 2009.
When exactly, depends on the next budget we don't know yet.
If it turns out that the situation on the political stage is changing, and politicians should be willing to save more, it could take until 2010.
But I don't expect such a change," he noted.
Debties grow
The national debt stems from the debts of the state government and comes mainly from the accumulation of deficits in the state budget.
The national debt is financed by treasury instructions, government bonds, direct bonds or, for example, bonds issued by the European Investment Bank.
Domestic debt has now risen to 719.4 billion kronor this year.
The state's foreign debt, meanwhile, rose by 1.7 billion kronor to 123.3 billion.
The so-called marketable sovereign debt reached 803.3 billion crowns at the end of September and fell by 13.7 billion since June.
The average maturity of national debt increased to 6.5 years.
"Total debt rose 13 per cent over the year from the first to the third quarter, which is really much given how well the economy is doing.
It is interesting that the growth is mainly seen in bonds, where the increase is 20 percent.
It's bad that the debt is rising, but on the other hand this shows that you can do something even before pension reforement comes," Pikora commented on the data.
Better than expected
It is clear from the financing strategy and the handling of the national debt for 2007 that the government had planned for this year to include 159.2 billion crowns.
At the beginning of December, however, thanks to the better development of state budget policy, the ministry reduced the plan to 152.8 billion crowns.
This amount stems mainly from the government budget deficit, which this year will obviously reach 76 billion crowns instead of the estimated 91.3 billions.
In addition, these are a maximum budgeted funds for the consolidation agency of 13.3 billion kronor, a ten-billion-dollar reserve for the payment of pensions, the financing of government bonds for 43 billions and installments for the bonds at the European Investment Bank in the amount of 1.8 billion.
For giving a teddy bear a name, she was presented to a dish in Khartoum today.
According to her lawyer, the British teacher, who had been charged with insulting Islam in Sudan, was sentenced on Thursday evening by a court in Khartoum to 15 days imprisonment and subsequent expulsion.
The British woman has been accused of allowing six- to seven-year-old pupils to give Mahomet the name to a teddy bear, even though any depiction of the Islamic prophet is prohibited.
She deserved to be flogged.
After parents of pupils complained several times to the Sudanese Ministry of Culture, the 54-year-old elementary school teacher, who worked at Khartoum's Unity High School, was picked up at home on Sunday and, according to Article 125 of the Sudanese Criminal Code, was accused of insulting Islam.
In fact, this matter begins in September, when the teacher asks her six- to seven-year-old students to pick out a name for the graduary.
"The children propose eight names, including Hassan, Abdullah, and Mohammed," said Robert Boules.
After all, when the class votes, 20 out of 23 children choose Mohammed as their favorite first name.
Surely, Mohammed is the name of the Islamic prophet, but above also a very frequent first name in Sudan.
Afterwards, the children created a book for the class, with the photo of the bear on it and the caption My name is Mohammed.
The bear himself had no shield with this name, but the problem is quite simply that the depiction of the Islamic prophet is seen as an insultation of Islam.
One of Gillian Gibbon’s Moslemic colleagues, who teaches at the same school in Sudan, declared that he knew her well, and she would never have done anything to come too close to a religious faith, “I only regret that she asked the children to vote for a name.”
An opinion shared by numerous students of the teacher, who describe them as “very attentive to their pupils” and “person who never speaks about religion in the class”.
Keep in mind that the first name Mohammed is also at the same time the second name given in Great Britain, for which there are 14 different spellings in English.
This first name, often chosen by Muslim families, is considered to be a kind of deference to the prophet (it is often given to the first-born of a family).
But what are the rules in relation to the use of this first name?
For example, can he be given to a toy?
This, of course, is a matter of interpretation.
On Wednesday, there was no agreement on the provincial status of the province at the last negotiating meeting in Vienna.
At the end the failure
The Serbs and Albanians from Kosovo ended a sixth and final discussion session Wednesday morning in Baden, Austria, without overcoming their controversies about the future status of the separatist province.
"It is unfortunate that the parties could not come to any agreement on the status," explained the European mediator Wolfgang Ischinger at a press conference in Vienna.
Emphasizing that peace in the region was at stake called on Frank Wilsner Belgrade and Pristina to fulfill their commitments to maintain peace, and to continue the talks beyond the end of the negotiations.
“No acts of violence are expected in the immediate future, but we are at the beginning of a very difficult time,” he added, suggesting that there are quite clear tensions.
End of mediations on December 10
The mediators of the Troika, which unites the European Union, the U.S. and Russia, will meet one last time in Serbia and Kosovo on Monday.
The future of the province, populated to 90% by Albanian-speakers, could become clearer if Ischinger, Wilsner and Russian mediator Alexander Botsane-Kharchenko submit their report to the U.N. Security Council by December 10.
The talks of Baden, near Vienna, formed the sixth and last negotiating session since the troika had received a mandate from the U.N. for a new mediation last August.
Americans and Europeans reckon their mediation order will end on December 10.
The Russians, who had already blocked Kosovo's approval of Kosovo's independence at the Security Council, insist on a continuation of the negotiations beyond that end date.
"Russia was and remains an advocate of a negotiated solution, a consensual solution," Botsane-Khartchenko said.
Unilateral Declaration of Independence?
After Kosovo has been under the administration of the UN for eight years, the Western countries consider independence under EU supervision to be the only solution with a future.
Pristina is prepared to announce it unilaterally, if the Security Council should not decide in their favor.
Belgrade insists on greater autonomy
"Regrettably, no agreement could be reached," the president of Kosovo's autonomous authority, Fatmir Sejdiu, announced at the hotel in Baden, where these "negotiations of the last chance" have been conducted since Monday.
Hashim Thaci, former leader of the Kosovo rebellion and future prime minister of the province, shared his regrets.
He added, however, that Belgrade's proposed plan on autonomy "belongs to a past that led to murders and deportations."
Domino Effect
Serbian President Boris Tradic, for his part, was of the opinion that a compromise should always be sought, and warned the Albanian majority from Kosovo against the consequences of a unilateral declaration of independence of the province.
Belgrade thinks that it is particularly a "domino effect" in a region that is still weakened so much by the wars of independence of the 1990s.
“For Serbia, this doesn’t mean a compromise would be impossible,” he said.
On Tuesday, the Serbian delegation insisted that any decision would be made solely solely in the Security Council, where its Russian ally held veto power.
“Kosora’s parliament will have the final say”
"Only a decision from the ranks of the Security Council is acceptable to Serbia," Serbian Prime Minister Vojislav Kostunica said.
"These negotiations have taken two years.
They have to end the way they started: in the Security Council.
Fatmir Sejdiu replied to the journalists that “Kosovio’s parliament will have the final say on status,” adding that any unilateral declaration of independence on the part of the Albanian-speaking population will proceed “in coordination” with Western countries.
Bush elicites an agreement between Israelis and Palestinians
At the opening of the international peace conference, which had gathered in Annapolis, Maryland on Tuesday on Tuesday, President Bush announced that Israelis and Palestinians had finally agreed on a joint statement.
He went on to explain that Israeli Prime Minister Ehud Olmert and Palestinian President Mahmoud Abbas had agreed to immediately begin negotiations in order to find a definitive settlement of the 60-year-old disputes between their two peoples.
In the presence of the two men, Bush promised that he would do everything he could to help them conclude a "peace agreement" before the end of 2008.
In the meantime, the Palestinians will have to destroy “the terrorist infrastructures” and Israel must cease its colonization work, Bush said, recalling the two parties of their commitments to the “roadmap” for the peace they agreed to in 2003.
The head of the White House emphasized that the negotiations between Israel and the Palestinian authorities under Abbas ultimately seek a peace treaty to clarify all outstanding issues without exception, including the central issues.
This designation includes the three serious problems around which the disputes between Israelis and Palestinians revolve mainly: the future borders of Palestine, the status of Jerusalem and the fate of the Arab refugees who were expelled in the course of the 1948 conflict from their homeland, which is located in the current state of Israel.
According to the American president, Abbas took the floor and insisted that the coming negotiations will be conducted "comprehensively and thoroughly" and provide a settlement for all points, enumerating, among other things, Jerusalem, the refugees, the borders, the colonies, water and security.
When Olmert, for his part took the floor, he pledged that the coming armistice negotiations would be conducted “directly and without interruption” and that he would make every effort to ensure that they could be terminated before the end of 20018.
Bush has set the deadline for the implementation of his “vision of two states,” a Palestinian one, and an Israeli one that coexiste next to each other, for the end of 20018.
Saudi Arabia has disclosed that it has received an assurance from Bush in this sense before it decided to attend the conference.
Arabia, whose participation in Annapolis is a great success for Washington, has launched a plan adopted by all Arab countries that offers Israel peace, in exchange for its withdrawal from all Arab territories that had been occupied during the 1967 war, including East Jerusalem.
Annapolis represents a crossroads in the history of the Middle East, especially because it attacks the roots of the conflict and its central points of view, says the Saudi-Arabic prince in his interview with the Arabic-language daily newspaper, which is financed by Saudi funds.
According to the public prosecutor, the police officers are not responsible
Exactly what happened on Sunday?
As always with this kind of local news, official versions and rumours face each other.
What are the circumstances of the accident?
According to the initial findings of investigations by the National Disciplinary Department of Police (IGPN), a police van drove in the direction of Villiers-le-Bel on Sunday, shortly after 5 p.m.
"He didn't have an assignment, but was on patrol, and drove at 40-50 km/h, without blue light," explains a spokesman for the police.
A green motorcycle of the Kawasaki brand, “without approval, not approved for public road traffic” (see below), was approached “at a very high speed” by two young steers without a helmet.
“It rammed the left side of the police car after it had disregarded its right of way from the right,” a police officer said.
The alcohol control carried out in the vehicle passengers is much negative.
The condition of the car (depleted fender, splintered windshield, torn bumper) testifies to the sharpness of the impact.
Very quickly, as early as yesterday morning, Marie-Thérèse de Givry, the Prosecutor of the Republic of Pontoise, announced that she had entrusted to the IGPN an investigation into the "facts of involuntary personal murder and the lack of assistance to people in danger," in coordination with Val-d'Oise's main district management.
In the afternoon, on the occasion of a press conference, the public prosecutor stated that she ruled out investigatory of the IGPN at this stage the responsibility of the polices.
According to the magistrate, three witnesses to the accident, who were heard Sunday evening, "the version of the police officers, namely that this small motorcycle has approached their left side relatively quickly".
Yesterday showed a kind of groove, certainly caused by the friction of footrests on the ground, that the two vehicles had stayed hooked into each other until the cops managed to brake.
The vehicles have been seized and will be the subject of a technical review.
The bodices of the two victims were autopsied yesterday.
What happened after that?
Yesterday in Villiers-le-Bel, a rumor quickly circulated, according to which the polices would have left the scene of the accident very quickly, without providing assistance to the two teenagers.
Although IGPN remains quite cautious, it would also rule out "any serious error" with regard to this matter.
According to a police source, “this is a difficult aspect of the investigation, for which further investigations are necessary, and in which you have to proceed very carefully”.
Libération met an eyewitness of those few moments shortly after the accident.
From one of his windows, he had direct views of the scene.
"We immediately went out with my cousin, who is a nurse in the hospital, to try to revitalize her, says this witness.
There was nothing to do.
Then he saw "a completely disturbed compolitan who limped.
Two others tried to revitalize one of the youngsters, the fourth on the phone.”
In view of the tension that had quickly formed around what happened and, it seems, due to the certainty that the people present had about the unexpected situation of the youths, the policemen were taken by colleagues who had arrived at the scene of the accident in the car.
According to this witness statement, the rescue workers arrived "about twenty minutes after leaving the polices".
They have also used heart massages, but in vain.
“I won’t let it be argued that the police forces didn’t provide assistance to the youths.
These young people were given first aid.
Firefighters arrived immediately.
Everything has been done to try to save her," the prosecutor insisted.
In Pakistan, tension is rising
On Monday, Pakistani police crushed the first demonstrations, since two days earlier the state of emergency had been declared by General Musharraf.
According to witnesses in the city of Lahore in the east of the country, about 2,000 lawyers who demonstrated against the measures taken in the context of the state of emergency were injured by the tear gas grenades used by the police there.
Those then threw with stones, with which they injured the policemen.
At least 350 lawyers have been temporarily detained.
There were similar scenarios in Karachi and Rawalpindi, where policemen attacked and injured lawyers.
Several would have been temporarily detained.
In Islamabad, lawyers from the Supreme Court's lawyers called for a general strike and announced demonstrations as a result of a wave of about 500 arrested by opposition activists, advocates of human rights and lawyers during the weekend.
Monday morning, the streets leading to the Supreme Court had been closed to prevent the assembly.
Further demonstrations had taken place in Multan (centre) and Peshawar (northeast).
On Monday, the lawyer movement received the support of the religious parties united in the powerful Muttahida Majlis-e-Amal alliance, whose president "called on the population to send flowers to the judges against the state of emergency, and those who took an oath before Musharraf.
Musharraf's promise no longer applies
Pakistan's Supreme Court is directly affected by the special measure announced Saturday evening.
He had to take a position on the legality of President Musharraf's re-election in a direct ballot on October 6 in the days that followed.
The chairman of the Court, Iftikhar Mohammed Chaudhry, was removed from office and superseded.
"All that we're representing is illegal, anti-constitutional, and contrary to Supreme Court decisions," The News said.
Pervez Musharraf's promise to make the overcommand over the army and become a civilian president "no longer valid" since invoking the state of emergencie, the deputy minister of information Tariq Azeem said on Monday.
He was expected to hand over his military uniform by November 15, date on which his first term as president ends.
Vice-Minister for Information has also confirmed that January's elections could be postponed.
In addition, the Pakistani government denied the persistent rumours in the country, according to which President Musharraf would have been assigned a whereabouts by his deputy army commander.
More than a third of pregnancies aren’t planned
This calculation takes into account the unwanted pregnancies (24%), as well as poorly planned or unforeseen births.
Even today, 40 years after adoption of the Neuwirth Act, which allowed contraception in France, one in three pregnancies (36%) is not planned, according to a study by the National Institute for Demographic Studies (Ined).
This calculation takes into account the unwanted pregnancies (24%), as well as poorly planned or unforeseen births.
The Ined estimates that the various methods of contraception, such as the pill or the spiral, still have a failure rate of 5-6%.
According to the institute, which finds that the percentage of pill users between 20 and 40 years of age who use the pill has risen from 5% in 1970 to 60% today, liberalization of contraception has nevertheless enabled better “control of fertility”.
In any case, eight out of ten births are carefully planned nowadays.
With one in five couples even accurately calculated for the month, and, if possible, in spring, the season in which it seems most pleasant to have a child.
Spectacular repair at the space station
Astronaut Scott Parazynski and his comrades from the crew of the International Space Station (ISS) will have taken more than seven hours in space to carry out the spectacular repair of a defective solar panel essential for the continuation of scientific work on the station.
Due to its duration, the removal of habitable modules, the speed with which it was organized and the tools “made” by the astronauts to carry out the repairs, has achieved several records.
The problem unfolded on Wednesday, when the astronauts unloaded the Harmony module from space shuttle Discovery, a large cylindrical part that is soon scheduled to accommodate European and Japanese scientific labs.
This module is a kind of landing bridge for the module laboratories.
But you have to be able to supply them with electricity.
So the crew's second task was to implement a 17.5-tonne module for generating electricity via solar panels that was already on the ISS in order to bring it to its definitive position.
After she had moved it, the crew set about the restructuring of the solar panels of the module, the span of which is 76 meters.
And it happened that one of the collectors, which can be unfolded like accordions, was damaged by a clamped cable over a length of about one meter
Restructuring was halted so as not to damage the collector even more.
The astronauts, who were originally supposed to leave space shuttle Discovery for one scrutiny and then repair another solar collector whose rotation system was faulty, were forced to dedicate themselves to the damaged collector.
And Scott Parazynski was selected to walk around at the end of the 30-metre-long remote-controlled arm of the space shuttle that had been attached to the station to carry out the repair.
He's one of NASA's best "spacewalkers."
Large, resistant, very skilful, he has a well-developed torso and long arms.
The 46-year-year-old trained doctor was therefore selected to “operate” the “injured” solar collector.
Having carefully inspected the damage, he severed the faulty cable and attached some kind of rails on the damaged site.
He was assisted by a second "Spacewalker" who remained near the lock, and, from inside, by the other astronauts, among them Peggy Whitson, commander of the crew.
The tool he used, a mini hockey racket with insulator wrapped to prevent an electric shock on the solar panels that generate several hundred volts, the astronauts on board the station had devised and "tinkered."
When the action was over, Scott Parazynski waited until the collector was fully unfolded before counting off his tools and returning to the station.
Yesterday afternoon the astronauts enjoyed half a day’s relaxation, before seven astronauts returned to the space shuttle Discovery today to fly back to Earth on Wednesday.
Title EADS sanctioned on the stock exchange
After the aviation and defense group announced that it will have to spend between 1.2 and 1.4 billion euros in order to counteract the arreargences of the A400M, EADS currently shows a significant decline on the Paris stock exchange.
EADS currently loses 4% to 21.96 euros on the Paris stock exchange.
The Group was forced to re-examine its business forecasts for 2007 with reference to the arrears caused by the slow-moving development of the engine of the A400M, which are delaying the maiden flight of the aircraft.
The costs announced in October to compensate for the delay in the delivery of their military transport aircraft amount to "between 1.2 and 1.4 billion euros, of which more than one billion for Airbus," the parent company of the European manufacturer EADS proclaimed.
As a result of these costs, EADS will be forced to announce new operating performance targets (EBIT) for 2007 when presenting its quarterly results next Thursday, he added without further details.
The group also indicates that the assessment of the new cost is related to the six-month backlog.
As a reminder, EADS's business performance has fallen from 86% in 2006, largely due to the costs associated with the delay of another program, that of the very large A380 carrier aircraft.
Originally, the group had envisaged an initial delivery of the A400M to the French army for October 2009.
Choosing the status quo
On Sunday it will not be exciting when counting the polls: the mass of Russians will choose President Putin's party, United Russia.
After a very sealed election campaign, she confirms her quest for stability, even authoritarianism.
Three days before the parliamentary elections, Russian President Vladimir Putin sends a message broadcast on the public channels to the voters, which has already been recorded, but whose contents are kept secret.
He is doing so in his capacity as a top candidate of the United Russia, the pro-Kremlin party, for which an overwhelming victory is announcing itself.
Yesterday, the president didn't let anything look through when he received the foreign ambassadors, and was content to provide a new point of attack against the Western powers accused of interference - his favorite topic during the election campaign.
"We know the price of a true democracy and hope for honest elections, extremely transparent and open, elections without fraud," he added.
From the point of view of the opposition, they misjudged.
She denounces an election campaign tainted by suppression.
Communist leader Guennadi Ziouganov says there have never been such dirty elections, not even in Eltsin.
After the suppression of the demonstrations last weekend, ex-chess world champion Garry Kasparovder was sentenced to five days imprisonment for unauthorized demonstration, according to his lawyer continued to be banned.
“The Russians have preserved the mentality of Homo Sovieticus”
After these parliamentary elections without tension, two much further questions arise:
 Who will succeed Putin as president?
Term yesterday announced the date of the first ballot of the presidential elections, March 2, 2008; the candidates have until December 23 to make themselves known.
Since he was elected in 2000 and re-elected in 2004, Vladimir Putin cannot run for a third mandate in a row.
And now there's no successor.
What role will Putin play after March?
Prime minister?
Chairman of the parliamentary majority and the almighty unified Russia party, which will select government and president?
Whatever it will be, in the parliamentary elections or in the presidential election, the previous power will triumph.
That's tradition.
"The Russians have retained the mentality of Homo Sovieticus, they are not masters of their fate," says sociologist Lev Goudkov, director of the Levada Independent Center.
Historian Nikolai Kopossov of the State University of Saint Petersburg goes back even further: bourgeois society has developed very slowly in this country, which has been Christianized five centuries after Europe; the famous community spirit has delayed the development of individuality.
After the liberal reforms of the 1990s, Putin reconnected with the model of a paternalist state, which has been fully embraced by the population, all the more so since this new paternalism was not followed by terror, but a certain economic prosperity, says Maria Lipman of the Carnegie Foundation.
Today, the Russians “given away from personal responsibility”, which they briefly demonstrated after the disintegration of the USSR.
What longer think about?
This phenomenon affects all walks of life, including the world of business.
And the administrative elite knows how to maintain the status quo: 65 governors (out of 85 Russian regional chairmen) cite the local electoral lists of the United Russia.
The new freedoms coming out of the West are often synonymous with chaos, as in the 90s.
The generality prefers stability.
In this sense, the Russians won't vote for the extremist parties either.
Conclusion by Tamara Tcherednitchenko, a 54-year-old professor: What longer to consider?
Those in power aren’t so bad as to replace them?
It's a heavy souvenir
After being kept confidential for more than 60 years, Bad Arolsen's Nazi archives, documenting the fates of 17.5-million victims of the Hitler regime, are now freely accessible.
The 47 million documents of the archive, which are housed over a total length of 27 km in Bad Arolsen, could not yet be viewed, unless with a “humanitarian” intention, i.e
In May 2006, the eleven states, including Germany, France, Israel and the United States, which were commissioned with their administration, agreed to make it accessible to researchers and historians.
After the ratification process of this decision has been completed, the opening has been valid since yesterday.
Delicate information
Historians' access to Bad Arolsen will make it possible to carry out investigations, "especially about the deportations of prisoners, the inmates of the concentration camps, and the state of health of forced laborers," explains Reto Meistern, head of these archives administered by the International Committee of the Red Cross (CICR).
Until recently, several states, including Germany, were against making these name-related documents available to the public due to the delicate nature of certain information.
Such as alleged homosexuality or the mental obstruction of some deportation victims.
The inmates of the concentration camps or the arrest warrants issued by Gestapo had been collected after the war for a practical reason: to find civilians who had been deported or accused of forced labour, and possibly to provide them with evidence of their arrest in order to pave their way to their compensation.
Even today, more than 300 permanent employees handle tens of thousands of personal enquiries in Bad Arolsen.
Nevertheless, part of the scientific community shows only a limited interest in access to Bad Arolsen.
“You can’t expect anything fundamentally new in these documents,” Pr. Wolfgang Benz, head of the research center for anti-Semitism at the Technical University of Berlin, explained in 2006.
The EU and India want to reach a free trade agreement in 2008.
At present, mutual trading between India and Europe already reaches 60 billion euros per year.
The EU is India's most important trading partner and accounts for 18% of its foreign trade.
European commerce minister Peter Mandelson and his Indian counterpart, Kamal Nath, said the European Union and India hope to sign a free trade agreement in 2008.
EU foreign ministers had givened the green light to the European Commission last April to open negotiations with a view to signing free trade agreements (ALE) with India, South Korea and the Association of Southeast Asian Nations (Asean). Mutual trade between India and Europe is already reaching around 60 billion euros a year.
The EU is India's first trading partner and accounts for 18% of its foreign trade.
The EU is also its first foreign investor, but according to the European Commission's figures, India is only the EU's ninth trading partner.
"A free trade pact will enormously advance the two economic areas" and "India would improve its access to a market of 450 million wealthy Europeans," Peter Mandelson confirmed.
Europe forms the first or second market for the ten products exported most frequently from India, the European Commissioner further explained. The Asian and European giants exchange goods worth 47 billion euros and services worth 10 billion euros every year, this week Daniéle Smadja, chairwoman of the deputy of the European Commission in New Deli Delhi, and regrets that mutual trading
(Source AFP)
The Bank of England takes note of the concern and offers 10 billion pounds for five weeks
The Bank of England on Thursday expressed concern about curbing the currently recurring concern about credit conditions, and announced an offer of ten billion pounds with a basic interest rate of 5.75% for five weeks, an offer with an unusual maturity.
In order to alleviate fears that the conditions on the financial market will be particularly tough at the end of the year, and in order to give banks greater certainty about the way in which they are supposed to organise their liquidity positions during this period, the bank intends to offer a significant portion of its savings deposits during the maintenance phase, which begins on December 6, in the form of an open-market operation for five weeks
The bank goes on to explain that in this way, 10 billion pounds (14 billion euros) will be circulated on December 6 at 12.15 GMT (14 billion euros), at the property tax rate and only until January 10.
This seems to be the first time that the BoE has circulated liquidities for five weeks in an open-market operation.
This usually happens for one week.
The BoE adds in its communication that it is "ready to take further measures" in order to obtain the "interest rates" different from day to day from day to day of the interbench loans with its basic interest rate.
The inter-bench interest rate different from day to day was 5.829% on Wednesday.
The three-month interest rate has settled to 6.6%, slightly higher since September 18.
Since the beginning of the financial crisis of the summer, sums circulated by the Bank of England have been linked to penal fees that the banks have refrained from taking advantage of them.
According to certain rumours, amplified by the development of the pound exchange rate in contrast to the euro, many preferred to sign for the sums put into circulation by the European Central Bank at more favorable interest rates and with safer discretion
UMP and NC refuse to review PS's proposals on purchasing power
On Thursday, the UMP and Nouveau Centre groups refused to discuss the articles of the socialist bill on purchasing power, amounting to a rejection of the text.
The text created in the context of a socialist «Nische (meeting on the initiative of a parliamentary grouping) indicated about ten measures that had been adopted on Wednesday by the National Committee of the PS, the majority of which amounted 50% to the employment allowance, the reintroduction of the TIPP flottante, a transport cheque or the limitation of rents.
After UMP and NC had voted against the examination of the articles (180 votes to 115), the session was dissolved after the main discussion, which gave rise to partly fierce exchanges of words between the majority and the opposition, a few hours before Nicolas Sarkozy's televised contribution about purchasing power.
The leader of PS MP Jean Marc Ayrault announced that his group will present all of their proposals as an amendment to the bill for the correction of finance, which had been examined by the Assamblee last week.
A new festival is born
As a result of numerous rumours regarding the creation of a new music festival in Philadelphia by C3 Presents, the promoter/producer announces the Vineland Music Festival in New Jersey, in collaboration with Festival Republic by British producer Melvin Benn.
“It’s an honor for our company to work together with a person who, no more and no less, has served as a role model for the current festival movement in America,” Charlie Jones of C3 shared on Bilboard.com.
C3 hosts the Lollapalooza festival in Chicago and the Austin City Limits Music Festival.
Vineland, New Jersey, is a summer destination for camping holidays.
The festival will probably take place from August 8-10 on a 500-acre private farm about 40 minutes from Philadelphia.
The director of Festival Republic Melvin Benn, formerly known as Mean Fiddler, is one of the producers of the world's most prestigious festivals.
In 1989, he took over the management of the Reading Festival in the United Kingdom, which attracts around 80,000 people annually.
In 19999, Melvin Benn added the Leeds Festival to his portfolio, and then, in 2006, Festival Republic created the Latitude Festival at Henham Park in Suffolk.
Since 2002, Melvin Benn, in his capacity as co-owner, has also taken care of production, licensing concessions and organizational tasks of the Glastonbury Festival, which can now accommodate up to 177,500 people.
Charlie Jones of C3, who describes Glastonbury as the “most inspiring event” he’s ever attended, says his company and Melvin Benn’s work parallel next to each other to provide the area with a European-style festival event.
“We’ve chosen Philadelphia as a market and we’re working eagerly with the Fairmount Park Commission to create a festival in the city center of Philadelphia.
We conducted negotiations with the city and the commission was motivated and enthusiastic about this project.
Regrettably, we're stuck in political debates about the current administration, Charlie Jones continues.
"Fortunately, we've been able to discuss with Melvin Benn and we're making preparations to create a great festival in an extraordinary place that will ultimately benefit the population."
C3 Presents revived the Lollapalooza festival at Grand Park Chicago in 2005, having profiled itself together with the Austin City Limits Music Festival in the world of festivals in 2002.
The two events have each generated $9.8m and $11.8million in gross tax accounts in 2007.
These two festivals take place in an urban environment.
"Melvin Benn's European model is a rural festival, whereas ours are more likely to take place on urban green spaces," says Charlie Jones.
"The aim is to merge these two concepts in one way or another in order at the same time to give the impression of a rural festival on the European and an urban in the American way."
Vineland is located approximately halfway between Philadelphia and Atlantic City.
"Vineland's community, including the mayor, takes on the burden of this event with joy," Charlie Jones says.
The latter refuses to disclose the terms of the agreement, but he confirms "that they are sufficiently interesting to invest in such an event and include a festival that will hopefully have a long life."
Charlie Jones says the reception capacity of the festival is "very large," roughly according to the 80,000 people at Bonnaroo Music Festival in Tennessee.
He says that the invitations have been sent out to the groups, but that nothing could yet be confirmed when they were printed.
"Vineland will offer tasteful rock, but the music will be very diverse in the hope of attracting all sorts of viewers," Charlie Jones says.
His partners from C3, which has received the top independent promoter award of the last Bollboard Touring Award, are Charlie Walker and Charles Attal.
Montreal meets the Terminator.
It’s not easy to get the main platform for Hollywood movies to Montreal, if none other than Terminator opposes it.
These lure attempts are part of the delegate duties.
A politically "sensitious mandate" because the governor of California, Arnold Schwarzenegger, denounces the "Runaway Productions."
Is it difficult to negotiate with the "governor"?
"You have to be careful, because it's the industry that decides where it's going.
If she finds that the conditions in Montreal are favorable, she'll go there.
Our role is to offer the ones you consider to be the best," he says.
Run to the festivals.
For Denis Turcotte, however, the promotion of the local cinema is a top priority at the moment.
Hunting for the Oscars has begun.
At all festivals on the West Coast, though that of Sundance, that of Denver, Seattle, or San Francisco, it's going to be about introducing the films from Quebec, assessing them and making sure the producers give interviews.
The dark age had a successful performance at the 30th Mill Valley Film Festival a few weeks ago.
He will be unveiled at the Palm Springs International Festival in January.
Shake hands with the Devil is on the poster in Santa Monica and will be unveiled at the 30th Starz Denver Film Festival in November.
This month, Quebec has also unveiled four long and four short films at the American Film Institute Fest.
Is the stock market imminent an end-year rally? Disconnoxience among experts due to U.S. mortgage crisis
Investors are already rubbing their hands with just under five weeks until the end-of-the-year financial statements on the stocket in view of the coming final year rally.
Nevertheless, the experts are not able to make reliable forecasts in this regard.
Banif believes that “purely statistically, the month of December on the stock market is usually accompanied by a positive market development
Nevertheless, it is not advisable to make forecasts in the short term and only on the basis of statistics. Especially not when serious tensions determine current market events
Although other experts do not approve of such a hefty increase in prices in the last few weeks of the year, they nevertheless note that “the stock market price is determined by the given liquidity of the market and the market participants, which, due to the lack of secure equity funds, invest mainly in pension funds, which is severely affected by the value-adding trend towards high-yielding assets.
As a result, according to these experts, the stock market is more likely to have a rather lateral price trend influenced by market fluctuations, “particularly because we can’t yet estimate the extent of the U.S. Federal Reserve’s possible decline in interest rates, an uncertainty that is mainly due to the increased crude oil price and the risk of the rise in the rate of inflation.”
Javier Barrio, chairman of the sales department at BPI in Spain, agrees that it is time to announce an imminent market rally.
"The chances are not very good," assures the expert.
Spain's Ibex stock index has already lost a value of 4% since its highest mark on November 8 from 15,945.7 points.
However, not all experts share the same negative opinion.
According to Juan Solana, an analyst at Intermoney, as a result of the restructuring of mutual funds and the traditional subsidy of the pension fund made each year at this time, the Ibex 35 could successfully complete the year with almost 16,000 points.
Nevertheless, the expert emphasizes that this year things could develop a little differently; mainly due to the end-year rally already taking place in the second week of December, and therefore directly after the bridge day on the day of the constitution.
According to the analyst, price fluctuations would not affect market trends.
According to Federico Olucha, an analyst at Bank Inversis, the assessments on the market situation so far are at an almost technical level, which could lead to “a deviation from the index values could soon become apparent, depending on the consequences of the US mortgage market crisis.
The analyst trusts that these are positive.
The analysts agree on the selection of values that are intended to drive Ibex forward to new ceilings.
Solana of Intermoney asserts that “this year it’s the big values.”
Among the value winners, the expert would include the two major banks and some real estate companies, “since the electricity sector will be too expensive at the appropriate time.”
Meanwhile Federico Olucha of Bank Inversis assures that the rises in connection with the banking and industrial sectors will come.
If the rally actually occurs, the real estate companies will benefit from it.
“ACS has been catching up in recent months and we hope that other real estate companies such as Ferrovial and FCC will take the same path,” he said.
With regard to the increased consumer behaviour during the Christmas days, periodic consumption will determine market events in the last half of the year.
On the other hand, the expert believes that the technologies will also achieve positive sales results, even if they are only represented in small numbers on the Spanish market.
Five times in seven years
In the last seven years, December only showed declining price declines in two cases.
At the end of 2003, when the technology bubble burst, this led the stock market out of its crisis and at the same time initiated the last real end-of-year rally.
This year, in which the Ibex 35 caught up by 28.17 percentage points, December showed a price increase of 6.68%.
It will become clear whether 2008 will be the fifth consecutive year (the Ibex has risen by 8.36% since January) and will also be able to demonstrate an end-year rally.
Popular will have its 200 credit institutions opened 12 hours a day from Monday to Friday.
Popular has decided to modernize much of its stores.
A total of 200 of them are located in the economically strongest regions of Spain.
For the institute, which operates under the brand name Popular Max, these branches represent a major milestone.
But also for the rest of the Spanish institutions, as Popular is the first banking group to take the plunge to extend the opening hours of its branches on weekdays until 20.30h on weekdays and start on Saturdays from 10h to 20.30h.
The banking group BBVA, which also repeatedly announced the extension of its opening hours in the branches, has not yet made a definitive decision.
The bank's current negotiations with the trade unions seem to be aimed at an agreement in which 75% of all branches will be closed on Saturdays and open on a weekday in the afternoon, although not for customers.
Whether further banks join the new initiative, which adapts to business opening hours, will depend on Popular Bank's experiences.
And that's exactly the intention that Popular pursues with her project.
The aim is to extend the opening hours of those branches which, due to its location, can cover the buoyant customers of independent, small and medium-sized companies, but also those of large department stores.
The bank wants to open the new 200 branches over the next 18 months, as they are not new, but changed opening hours.
For this purpose, the bank will redesign the premises of those branches that are already located in business zones and at the same time align them with the individual customer profile of the different zones.
Thus, the extension of opening hours is combined with a new interior design of the branches, which is characterized by a more up-to-date, more colorful design.
Ángel Rivera, the bank representative responsible, assures that the restructuring does not require any major investments, but would be accompanied by an increased staff recruitment, since a branch no longer needed six, but eight or nine employees.
The branches, which will even be equipped with additional premises for the SMEs, will not sell financial products.
A positive experience for the group
Popular has already tested its concept at nine stores in Madrid.
And the levels of experience were positive, according to Ángel Rivero.
The branches of the streets Bravo Murillo, O ́Donell and Gran Vía have been selected, which seems to have been a wise decision.
Popular currently manages about 2500 branches.
And another 60 who operate under the name Mundocredit. These are specialized in immigrants and also have extended opening hours, even on Sundays and holidays.
PopularMax stores don't use the BBVA's model, as most of them will offer a wide range of non-financial services for sale instead.
“We don’t want to compete for our customers,” Rivero explains.
With Telefonica, they have provisionally come to the agreement to sell mobile phones with the help of external bank personnel.
For the first time, the Spanish private channel Telecinco is doubling its stock market value and thus overtakes its rival Antena 3.
“We made it.”
With this sentence, the managing director of Telecinco, Paolo Vasile, announced his satisfaction at the close of the stock market. The broadcaster led by him had increased his value by two times his rival Antena 3.
However, one aspect dampened his joy: This is exactly what hurts me; not that we have higher growth, but that we had lower losses,” he noted.
He emphasized that, despite the weak market, “they didn’t sunk too low.”
Telecinco ended the day yesterday with an increase of 2.54%.
This gain reduces its previous losses to 15.66% so far.
According to Bloomberg, goodwill now reaches 4,488.88 million euros, with Mediaset controlling the main share of capital (52%), and Vocento is only involved with 13%.
Rival Antena 3 lost 1.52 percentage points yesterday and has recorded a total loss of 40.5% since January, bringing its market value to 2,193.46 millions to half.
The market value of other listed communications companies, on the other hand, rises to 3,571 million in the case of Sogecable, to 2,518.53 million at Prisa (publisher of Cinco Días magazine) and to 1,779 million at Vocento.
The comparison with Antena 3 can be derived from the faculty that “two similar companies,” according to Vasile.
Uncertainally, the question of to what extent we can actually act in terms of exchange, at what price and how we can make it lucrative at the customer level.
Telecinco announced a week ago its intention to become the first TV channel with more than 1000 million gross profits from advertising revenue this year. Guiseppe Tringali, the managing director of Telecinco and Publiespaña, the group's advertising company, assured.
If the project were to be implemented, the broadcaster would increase its advertising income by 3.6% compared to the 965.4 million from the previous year.
In the case of Antena 3, net sales increased by 1.6% in the first nine exchange months (up to 734.4 million), while net revenues from the television business could only show an increase of 0.7% (630.4 million ).
The analysts see the main factor for Telecinco’s better stock market trend in the high payout ratio, which the company has been able to show up to now: In 2006, the company, led by Alejandro Echevarría, initiated a 100 percent dividend payout.
Vasile, however, assures that the ratio could still vary after the purchase of 33% of Endemol's shares, with Mediaset as its parent company, even if it is still too early for an official announcement.
“We are obligated to the official compromise to pay out at least 50% of the shares, even if we’ve always stated substantially more.
I hope that we can satisfy our shareholders this year, whose share values we have already been able to increase by buying 33% of Endemol shares.
According to analysts, the market is currently experiencing certain loss trends.
Lehmann Brothers only recently reduced its profit calculation at Telecinco by 4% this year and by 7% for 2009.
This is the consequence of the reduction in the expected growth rate, which will decline from 10.5% to 8.4% in 2008 and from 5.5% to 5.1% in 2009.
This has also downgraded Antena 3's estimated profit calculation by 4.5% for 2008 and by 7.5% for 2009.
