A colleague informs about the inclusion of illegal Internet downloads in the criminal code:
The download of a song would be a violation of the law, which would be affected with six months imprisonment.
However, if you rob a music store below a value of 400 euros, this is only considered a simple violation (Article 623.1).
B. If you rob the business up to a value of 1000 euros, then the theft is classified as a criminal offense, but the penalty is less than with a download (Article 234 of the Criminal Code).
C. If you smash a car and return it forty hours later, then the penalty is still less than with a download (article 244.1).
D. It's less serious to participate in a brawl than in a music data exchange.
Participating in a rebellious dispute entails a prison sentence of three to 12 months (Article 154 of the Penal Code), while the exchange of data results in deprivation of imprisonment from six months to two years
(Article 270 of the Criminal Code).
E. According to Article 184.2 of the Penal Code, sexual harassment in the workplace is considered less bad than the downloading of a DVD film from the Internet.
F. The trade in copyright-protected films, the authorization of which has not taken place by the authors, is subjected to a higher penalty than the distribution of pornographic footage to minors (Article 186).
Well, well, if my colleague was right, then someone screwed up his demands.
4.
My family doctor presents me with one of his long, strange research articles titled: “Money leads to activation of certain brain areas, especially when the others get more of it.”
So the feelings caused by the monthly salary payment depend on the salaries of the colleagues.
The study was prepared using a tomographic-magnetic reference analysis at the University of Bonn, and the results were published in the last issue of the science journal.
According to Bernd Webers, author of the study and director of the Bonn Brain Research Center Life&Brain, “the activation was strongest when the participant gave correct answers and his teammate failed.”
The researchers were able to realize that the brain activities were always very moderate if both participants were correct with their answer and therefore also received the same content; but if one of them received twice, there was also stronger brain activity.
I’m not quite sure if it actually requires so much scientific hassle to come to such a result.
Prosecutors from the Bal Balkans
Carla del Ponte has been relentlessly searching for the criminals of the Balkan Wars for eight years. Her official residence is in the public prosecutor's office of the criminal tribunal of the former Yugoslavia, based in La Haya.
Her mandate will expire at the end of the year and Carla will leave office with a crying and a laughing eye, as the massacrades of Srebrenica - in which eight thousand Bosnian Muslims died - namely the ex-General Ratko Mladic and the former Serbo-Bosnian leader Radovan Karadzic continue to be at large.
Their names are on list, the Swiss-born director Marcel Schüpbach as the template for the title of his documentary La Liste de Carla (2006, in Spain not yet); a film that with the tempo of a political thriller pursues the Swiss prosecutor from Belgrade to New York in her plan to track down the escaped criminals and to pressure the respective governments
Which insurance is best for me?
VEHC insurance, which covers a possible case of damage caused by the driver, involves a high cost, the amount of which in turn depends on various factors, e.g. on age, gender, time of preparation of the driver's license, marital status, type of car to be insured and its use, etc.
Owning a car is only the beginning.
Because it is even more difficult to find the right car insurance than the right vehicle model.
First of all, you have to be clear what we are dealing with here.
Car insurance is an agreement between an insurance company and a policyholder.
This agreement is stipulated in writing by means of an insurance contract, which stipulates that the company receives the agreed insurance premium and, in return, is obligated to submit the corresponding severance amount in the event of damage.
The insurance premium is the price of an insurance composed of several factors, including age, gender, time of preparation of the driver's license, marital status, type of the vehicle to be insured or its use.
The insurance companies determine their prices according to the statistics, so that in the event of a higher risk of accidents, higher contribution payments are also due.
For this reason, both 25-year-olds and women have to make fewer contributions, because statistics show that these groups have a lower accident rate thanks to their caution.
The same also applies to married persons, since, according to the statistical values and therefore also according to the conviction of the companies, to fewer accidents like single people.
The benefit attached to the vehicle impairs the frequency of driving and thus also the probability of an accident.
The more often the use, the more likely the damage.
In setting up the premium, the insurance companies have to take into account a whole range of points.
For this reason, the driver also has to thoroughly study the situation in order to select exactly the car insurance that is most likely to meet his personal needs.
At this point, we now list the most important points that should be considered when selecting insurance, in order to be able to make not only a performance-oriented but also a price-oriented choice.
The under 25-year-olds encounter numerous obstacles in the choice of low-cost insurance, such as the low driving practice and the "young" driver's license.
Even if it would be best for ideal insurance coverage to take out full comprehensive insurance, there are still cheaper alternatives.
The cautious drivers can select a full comprehensive insurance with a deductible, so that the services of a fully comprehensive insurance are covered, but offered at a low price.
This type of contract means that the customer has to pay part of the amount incurred as a result of the damage.
The amount is determined before the conclusion of the contract and based on the amount of the insurance premium, which is higher that the sum to be paid, explains Luis Noguera, Spain’s main representative at First Europa, an online insurancement agency.
Another option is extended insurance, which insures the customer against external accidental damage, but not against the damage caused to his own car.
In addition, this type of insurance would cover theft, fire and vehicle loss.
Here's some advice.
If you’re under the age of 25, it’s in your hands to reduce insurance costs; this will increase your credit by trying to drive cautiously and prevent accidents.
Credition is a reduction of the basic tariff at the relevant insurance company.
On the contrary to this, a penalty payment includes an increase in said tariff.
The amount of credits and penalties depend on society and its proceeds.
If the company has had a high-yielding year, it can allow itself to increase the bonus payments,” Noguera explains.
As soon as the 25-year mark is exceeded, it is easier for the drivers to conclude a contract. The reason for this is the fact that with increasing driving practice, the accident rate decreases.
Thus, it makes the most sense to opt for full comprehensive insurance with a deductible.
“Usually, young people can’t take advantage of their previous experience, both in the event of an accident and at the time of driving,” explains the Spanish head of the European company, adding that the majority of accidents occur in the second year of practice, after the beginner’s “fear” has been deposited.
Nevertheless, it is more advisable for a married customer who does not have children to take out comprehensive insurance; of course, only if the financial situation of the corresponding person also allows it.
On the part of insurers, married couples with children are considered the customers with the lowest accident rate, and their contribution payments are correspondingly low.
In this case, comprehensive insurance with a deductible (with an amount around 300 euros) would be the best option. In case you have received a high credit, an extended insurance is recommended, which is only used for the third vehicle year.
“They drive more relaxed overall and have a greater purchasing power that allows them to make a higher contribution payment,” Luis Noguera notes.
For pensioners, it is advisable to take out full comprehensive insurance with a deductible (with an amount around 150 euros), or an extended insurance, which only starts with a sufficiently high ceremony.
Ultimately, one has to state that car insurance is something very important, not only because it protects the driver against possible accidental damage, but also because it entails high costs, which require a thorough selection to be made corresponding to our personal situation.
Celtic Glasgow are hoping for the point for the eighth final in Milan
FC Milan and Celtic Glasgow will fight for the point they are lacking next Tuesday in San Siro to achieve their respective goals even before the conclusion of the Championsleague preliminary round. The Italians need the point to get to the top of their group, while the latter decides on their participation with the Scots.
The Italian squad already has five points and have already confirmed their participation in the fifth round with a draw in Portugal, while Celtic Glasgow, with nine points, still need another to make sure FC Shakhtar won't catch up with Befica Lisbon with four points.
The Group D two matches will take place one week before the end of the sixth round of play, as FC Milan will have to compete at the FIFA Club World Cup in Japan, where it will compete for the first in the semi-final for the first time, which takes place on the 16th.
So this group will be completed above all others and the draw at the Guiseppe Meazza Stadion to the all-round expected result, even if both teams will try to win. The Italians will not want to speculate at home the Keltik Glasgow knows that in the event of a victory they would be group first and would thus have a better starting position in the eighth final.
Gordon Strachan’s team could even suffice a defeat to qualify, but he shouldn’t rely too much on such circumstances, because the Ukrainians are entitled to participate in nine points; they lost last week in Glasgow (2-1), but were able to win in the second game (2-0).
Accordingly, the Milanders will try to spoil the Scot the evening, who in turn hope to reach the eighth final of the reshaped Champions League for the second time in a row, having been defeated in the previous season in exactly the same round by a goal from Kaka in post-match time (1-0) and by a zero-to-zero draw at the first leg.
As FC Shakhtar is waiting for Rossoneri victory, the squad will have to focus on their own game, as reaching the eighth final as well as their membership of UEFA will depend on its victory and defeat of Celtic Glasgow.
Benfica Lisbon, despite its lower table position and the prospect of continuing to participate in the biggest European championship, could take third place through a win and thus play for UEFA.
20,000 people are attending the first Spice Girls Tour concert in Vancouver.
The Spice Girls gave the go-ahead for their first tour after the band’s dissolution in 2000 in front of 20,000 spectators.
'Posh Spice' (Victoria Beckham), 'Scary Spice' (Melanie Brown), 'Baby Spice' (Emma Bunton), 'Sporty Spice' (Melanie Chisholm) y 'Ginger Spice' (Geri Halliwell) declared shortly before the beginning of their concert at GM Place de Vancouver that the mood in the reunified band
Around 20,000 people were able to witness the concert in the sold-out stadium, in which a video on the history of the band, which had caused a sensation at the end of the nineties, was shown.
Reunification and world tour overlap with the release of a new album, which includes their biggest hits and two other new songs, Headlines and Voodoo, which is released in Spain under EMI’s label.
The five members of the Spice Girls wore on stage golden armbands, which the husband of Posh Spice, the English footballer David Beckham, had given to them earlier.
The athlete found himself among the fan crowds in the audience that made the GM Palace glow, even though the nerves of his spouse Victoria had forbade him in the previous days.
During the preparations for the Schow Victoria Beckham announced that the British star "didn't see our cloakroom once, and that's why I'm quite excited to see what he's going to think of the performance."
Sporty Spice, whose withdrawal from the group in 1998 “due to internal disagreement” entailed the dissolution of the group two years later, expressed its nervousness two hours before the concert at a press meeting.
“I was shocked.
I was nervous, but I saw that the attitude of the world's public had changed, that the feelings about the Spice Girls had changed.
And that's why I didn't, so I'm glad to have made that decision," Chisholm commented on the break-up of the band that prompted it nine years ago.
The singer also announced that “the disputes have been eliminated and now everyone likes each other again.”
Nevertheless, many things have changed since Spice Girls' last joint appearance in 1998.
First of all, according to the TV channel CTV, the group arrived in a special aircraft of the Virgin Airlines in Vancouver.
Upon their arrival at the airport, the band was received by an army of nannies.
The five together have seven children.
After the concert in Vancouver, the band will next perform in California, in San José (on December 4th) and in Los Angeles (5th and 7th December).
In Madrid, they will take the stage on 23 December.
New Fears About Natural Gas Dispute Between Russia and Ukraine
A new price dispute smolders between Ukraine and Russia, which increases the risk that Russian exports of fuel to Western Europe will be affected.
Most of Russia's natural gas exports to the European Union (EU) are routed through Ukraine and any dispute between the two nations is watched very closely.
Kiev has warned that if Moscow increases the price it has to pay for natural gas, it will charge higher transit fees from Russia.
An earlier conflict between the two states last year reduced shipments to EU states.
The recent dispute between Kiev and Moscow happens at a time when the Ukrainian government and the Russian state gas monopoly Gazprom are trying to reach an agreement on how much Ukraine will have to pay for Russian natural gas in 2008.
According to Gazprom, Ukraine needs to be charged more, as the Group has agreed to pay up to 50% extra for natural gas from neighboring Turkmenistan.
Analysts say this could mean that Ukraine will have to pay 40% more for Gazprom's imports.
"It is important for us to use our arguments as one of the most important gas transit states during talks," Ukraine's economy minister Anatoli Kinakh told Reuters news agency.
"Changing natural gas prices should undoubtedly be linked to our arguments regarding the amount of transit fees [to Western Europe] via Ukraine."
In the last major natural gas dispute between Moscow and Kiev in January 2006, Gazprom cut supplies to Ukraine.
As a result, this led to a fall in exports to Western Europe, which drastically drove up prices.
Russian gas accounts for a quarter of the European Union's supply.
Russia has the world's largest gas reserves, but much of it remains underdeveloped.
As a result, Gazprom is supplementing its own supplies with gas from Turkmenistan and other Central Asian former Soviet nations.
NHS 'must control foreign workers'
The Scottish State Health Service needs to improve checks on criminal records for workers from outside Europe, a member of the supervisory body said. outside
The recommendation of chief financial controller Robert Black came after a foreign doctorate in Scotland was brought into connection with the attack on Glasgow airport in June.
Black also expressed concern that the Scottish state health service does not have accurate expectations regarding the number of its foreign employees.
But his report pointed out that other important checks were carried out correctly at a sample of five health authorities.
The main financial controller examined personnel recruited outsiding the countries of the European Economic Area as a result of the attack on Glasgow airport.
Bilal Abdullah, 28, an Iraqi doctor at the Royal Alexandra hospital in Paisley, was arrested at the airport after an all-terrain vehicle was driven through the doors of the main airport terminal.
He was one of three doctors who appeared at Old Bailey in a video transmission in October, and has been charged in connection with the suspected car bomb attack in the UK.
It is expected to carry out checks in the run-up to the employment of foreign employees to ensure that they possess the necessary qualifications, experiences and authorizations to work in the UK.
Although the health authorities use Disclosure Scotland to verify criminal records, the agency does not have the competence to obtain this information outside the UK.
The report of the main examiner now states, among other recommendations, that the board should take up guidelines of the criminal record excerpt, which helps employers with checks in different countries.
"Although we found evidence of these checks in some personnel records, these have not been carried out routinely," the report noted.
Black said Scottish health authorities would closely follow their investigation guidelines prior to employment, but added: "Our report shows areas where the system can be improved to ensure that accurate information about the number of foreign health service workers is available."
BMA Scotland chairman Dr Peter Terry said it was "extremely unlikely" that investigations in the run-up to the employment of potential NHS staff would identify people who could commit future terrorist attacks.
"The report states that current pre-employment tests by the NHS Scotland work well and it is not clear what added value the recommendations will deliver," he said.
Scottish health authorities say they currently have 1,161 foreign employees, the vast majority of whom doctors and nurses.
However, the financial controller warned that the number is likely to be higher, also because the boards would have trouble identifying those employees who are on leave indefinitely.
The inquiry continued that established control procedures of NHS authorities in Ayrshire, Arran, Grampian, Greater Glasgow and Clyde, Lanarkshire, and Lothian have been adhered to predominantly.
These include passport, work permit, and qualification checks.
The Scottish Government is currently reviewing its policy and guidelines on checks before the start of employment for foreign staff in the NHS.
Secretary of Health Nicola Sturgeon said: "It was a concern for me to carry out this work in light of the terrorist attack on Glasgow Airport."
It added that the Scottish Government, in conjunction with the health authorities, would take into account the recommendations of the report and to ensure that the best practices would be reflected in updated guidelines regarding the check-ups in the run-up to the employment of all staff - including those from abroad - whose publication is planned next month.
Joy division dominates film award ceremony
Control, the film biography about the late Joy Division singer Ian Curtis has won five prizes at the British Independent Film Festival, among others for the best film.
Prizes were also awarded to lead actor Sam Riley, who worked in a warehouse before being casted for the role of Curtis, director Anton Corbijn, and supporting actor Toby Kebbell.
Dame Judi Dench has been honored as best lead actress for the thriller Notes on a Scandal.
And Bond actor Daniel Craig won the Variety award for bringing the British film into the world public.
Prizes were handed over in a ceremony at London's Roundhouse.
Control follows Curti's rise to fame with Joy Division until his suicide in 1980 at the age of 23.
The black-and-white film - on whose soundtrack The Killers, David Bowie and New Order can be found - was filmed for just 3 million British pounds.
Nevertheless, he has contributed two prizes to the Dutch photographer Corbijn, so far best known for the iconic cover of the U2 album Joshua Tree - as best director and best directorial debut.
Rock music was the focal point of another laureate at the ceremony - when Julian Temple's film about Clash singer Joe Strummer received the award for best documentary.
The award for the best performer went to Viggo Mortensen for his role as the driver of a Russian mafia boss in David Cronenberg's London-making thriller Easter Promises.
Ray Winstone, who has a reputation for playing tough guys, has picked up the Richard Harris Prize for his outstanding contribution to British film.
The 50-year-old, whose films include Scum, Nil by Mouth and Sexy Biest, is currently filming the Indiana Jones sequel The Kingdom of the Chrystal Skull.
Winstone said he was proud to receive the award, which was named in honor of Camelot and Harry Potter Stars Harris, who died in 2002.
"It's been people like Richard Harris and Albert Finney who opened doors to people like me," he said.
"I've been lucky enough to meet Richard Harris a few times.
He's my kind of person, a great actor of his time, I'm really honored.'
The independent film awards, known as Bifas, are chosen by a group that includes filmmakers, actors and festival organizers.
Bifa supporters and sponsors include Mike Figgis, Ken Loach, Ewan McGregor, Samantha Morton, Bill Nighy, Meera Syal and Michael Winterbottom.
208 Arrested in Saudi Arabia over a terror plot
Saudi Arabia revealed yesterday that it arrested 208 militants because of their affiliation with cells, which had planned an imminent attack on an oil facility, as well as clergies and security forces.
The world's largest petroleum exporter's state television said one of the cells had planned to smuggle in missiles.
Sympathizers of Al-Qaeda have launched a campaign against the monarchy that has been an ally of the United States since 20303 as a result of the invasion of Iraq.
A cell of eight militants, led by a foreign citizen, planned an attack on an oil facility in the eastern provincial province, according to the television report.
Saudi Arabia has formed a 35,000-strong rapid response force to protect plants following a failed Al Quaida attack on the world's largest petroleum processing plant in Abqaiq in 2006.
The report, referring to an interial ministrian's statement, says 18 of those arrested belonged to a cell run by a "missile expert" who immigrated into the country illegally.
He reports that the militants had planned to smuggle eight projectiles into the kingdom.
According to the report, another 22 of the detainees intended to kill clergies and security forces.
The government has warned clergies to do more to stop Saudis who are aiming Iraq to partner with Al Qaida militias who want to fight the Allied forces and the U.S.-backed Shiite Muslim government, who is seen as heretical by the hard-liners among the Sunni Saudis.
Al Qaeda militias consider many clergies in Saudi Arabia to be cooperating with the authorities in supporting the politics of the royal family that dominates the government.
The militias - accompanied by appeals from Osama bin Laden, Saudi-born Al Qaeda leader to target the pro-Western Saudi government - are targeting foreign residential areas, government buildings and facilities of the energy sector.
"This has been a very great effort by the security forces over the last... five months," an interior ministry spokesman told state television.
The arrests also affected a "media cell" of 16 from Medina, which aimed to promote "Takfiri thinking" - the ideology of radical Sunni Muslims who support violence against Muslims who are branded as infidels and apostates.
Among those detainees were also 32 people accused of providing financial support to the militias.
The climate at Venus could reveal a view of Earth's future
Venus may once be a pleasant place for life, scientists said yesterday.
Datas of a European spacecraft located on an orbit show that the planet resembles an averted twin on Earth even more than was previously known.
Venus is Earth's closest planetary neighbor.
The two worlds have about the same size and have similar planetary orbits, but Venus is a little closer to the sun.
Billions of years ago there would have been conditions on Venus that would have been very similar to those on Earth and could even have been habitable, scientists believe today.
But because of the unchecked carbon dioxide greenhouse effect, the erosion of the atmosphere caused by sunlight and a slower pace of rotation, Venus embarked on a completely different development course.
Today, the planet is deprived of water and has a surface temperature that is hot enough to melt lead and is devastated by hurricane-strong winds and lightning storms.
The findings of the European Space Agency's Venus Express spacecraft, which examined the planet for a year, provide a sobering view of how the Earth could develop in the face of catastrophic climate changes.
Professor Fred Taylor of the Department of Physics at the University of Oxford, one of the heads of the Venus Express mission, said: "It becomes clear where the climate on Venus is so different from that of the Earth, whereas the planets are otherwise quite similar."
"Our new data makes it possible to construct a scenario in which Venus began billions of years ago like Earth, possibly with a habitable environment."
'Enriched uranium' seized by police
A kilo of radioactive material, reportedly enriched uranium, was seized by Slovak and Hungarian police yesterday.
A police spokesperson said that three people have been arrested and that the material will be scrutinized.
He didn't confirm whether it was enriched uranium, but said it could have been sold for 1 million dollars (480,000 pounds).
All still open
Mitt Romey said he didn't consider it worthy of a presidential nominee to be questioned by a snowman.
Even so, the Republican nominee for the presidency ultimately agreed to face a debate on YouTube in which regular voters record and submit questions on video to send them to the candidate.
None of the questions raised on Wednesday night, November 28, were as ridiculous as the snowman who asked Democrats about global warming in the other party's YouTube debate.
But the questions, and the answers, this time accurately reflected the state of the republican party that breaks into next year's elections.
It's not known, just like CNN's producers, who co-backed the debate that selected YouTube clips to be shown to the budding candidates.
However, in the first half-hour of the debate, which has been devoted the longest to a single issue, the focus was on immigration.
The issue is one of the most powerful issues for Republicans.
CNN asked a group of undecided voters to operate a dial when they heard things they liked: The televisory station noticed peaks of approval as the candidates spoke strictly about the borders.
The sharpest and longest exchange of the evening was between Rudy Giuliani, the national frontrunner, and Romney, who has moved forward in the crucial polls in Iowa and New Hampshire.
Giuliani is trying to shake off the reputation that he has been compliant about illegal immigration when he was mayor of New York.
He attacked Romney in which he accused the former Massachusetts governor of hiring illegal immigrants for work at his home.
The two put themselves apart particularly fiercely as questioners asked if any of the candidates would support an "amnesty" (which would give illegals a regular status and possibility of citizenship).
More insignificant candidates such as Duncan Hunter, a Californian congressman, and Tom Tancredo, a congressman from Colorado, managed to catch up on this issue.
Neither of the two men has serious chances in the nomination, but both stay in the race, perhaps because they've slapped the immigration taffles sooner and harder than others.
At least in the area of immigration, candidates broadly share the same views: sharper borders, intolerancy towards illegal migrants.
This issue, along with fears about toxic toys from China or other worries about the rest of the world, help Republicans forget or minimize the differences between liberals and Christian conservatives.
But the debate clearly became problematic when other issues were raised.
Mr Giuliani was asked why he had supported gun restrictions in the past.
John McCain and Romney discussed whether simulated drowning should be considered torture.
One of the questioners asked if the candidates believe every word in the Bible.
This led Giuliani to take the stand that even though the Bible was his favorite book, he might not literally believe the story of Jonah and the whale.
Another questioner asked whether Jesus would support the death penalty.
Mike Huckabee - now, contrary to all expectations in first place in a recent poll in Iowa thanks to his long-existing Christian conservativism - said the death penalty was painful but necessary.
When he was urged to decide whether Jesus would endorse them, he laughed about it, and said Jesus was too intelligent to apply for political office.
The race remains exceptionally wide open.
Huckabee's rise in Iowa coincides with Ron Paul's recent fundraising successes and his applause storms in Wednesday's debate.
These two are shot up out of nowhere because the party's two wings have so far been unable to agree on one of the better-known and traditional candidates.
In 1980 and 2000, a nominee has united the party from the outset, convincingly with a victorious message about America's size and a wink on both wings of the party.
This time, the party is divided and the result is far from clear.
Labour party's pain
Gordon Brown has discovered yet another pit to fall; his way out of it remains the same.
For British scandals, this one is not particularly slippery.
No noble titles seem to have been sold, no politician of Paris hotel bills paid, no extramarital toes sucked.
But the revelation that a rich businessman has obscured donations to the British ruling Labour party for more than 650,000 pounds (1.3 million dollars) over four years under the name of other people, and that the party has failed to report this one is something without which the contested Gordon Brown would have done well.
When two months ago the prime minister eluded the demand for an election, stupidly insulting the intelligence of the British by claiming that opinion polls had not influenced this decision, there was hope that he would provide a robust, efficient government.
Instead, as Vince Cable, the increasingly astuteous representative of the Liberal Democrats, put it, the once-dreaded Brown of Stalin has evolved into Mr. Bean.
The awkward rescue of Northern Rock, an ailing bank, showed regulatory weakness and poor judgment under fire.
Poorly thought-out proposals to amend tax laws have pushed business associations against the government.
The tax loss of personal data of 25 million people showed unimaginable administrative puffs.
A trio of retired generals launched an uprising in the upper house in protest against cuts in military spending: being ministers of the armed forces, they claimed, was a part-time job.
Now there are the diverse donations from David Abrahams; Peter Watt, Labour's secretary-general (and their former vicarious manager) has already resigned.
None of this means that Brown irrevocably sets off for resignation.
Still, governments can reach a turning point after which they find it impossible to govern.
People don't like to trust politicians, but they usually withhold their skepticism when a new troupe takes office.
If it is clear that it is unlikely that a prime minister will improve the situation, and he does not even need to be there for a long time, this postponement is over: the public service is beginning to leak; cabinet ministers are beginning to chat; the press is looking for bad news; and the government becomes defensive and aimless.
In one way, Brown's position is particularly difficult because, unlike other political leaders, he can't claim to be a new broom.
As much as he'd love to deny Tony Blair, he was his lieutenant-in-Chief.
The problems with Northern Rock and the tax jurisdiction records, for example, are related to decisions Brown made as chancellor of the Treasury.
And even if he wasn't directly involved in Blair's mishaps - as in the lengthy "money for nobility title" investigation into political party funding - voters potentially assume he was.
Still, Brown's location isn't quite as bad as she looks
The misconduct isn't as terrible as the Conservatives describe it.
Just like Mr. Bean, no one died, or has been completely swindled.
And the antidote, painful as it may be, is to deliver what Brown’s, of course, Stalinist tendencies have always promised: a reliable, solid and slightly brutal government.
The new fuss about political donations is a good example.
Garboesque Abrahams, who compares his donations to his charitable gifts, he does not wish public recognition for his benevolence.
This may be the case, but he was not entitled to the decision.
The laws against anonymity for major political contributors are clear and they exist for an unequivocal reason: the public has a right to know who can buy access to those who rule the country.
Watt, who says he would have known about Abraham's agreement, but it's not that it's illegal, will, of course, have to ask himself some questions; just as Jon Mendlesohn, Labour's main financial initiator, who was told about the strange donations in October.
Yet so far Brown isn't involved: there's nothing to suggest that he knew about these confusions, even though most of the payments were made after taking office in June.
So far, the prime minister has done half the job.
He quickly condemned the violation of the laws by his party, but he diluted that determination by grasping for a well-known instrument, an investigative committee on the issue - to make the measure full, by a retired bishop and a judge.
In this case, the "lessons to be drawn from it" aren't very complicated.
If the law was broken, it's a matter for the police.
Apart from that, there are other things Mr Brown needs to do, such as finding a permanent chairman for the Independent Committee on Standards in Public Life, who has been vacant for six months, and moving forward with a major reform of political party funding.
That would require Labour's hard-liners to cooperate with the Conservatives and possibly give up the special financial relationship with the unions - bitter pills for a partisan like Mr Brown.
But he should be aware of some partisan facts.
Conservatives now have clear leadership in opinion polls; he has two years of winning back voters; and the only way he can achieve victory is through unimpeachable righteousness and solid competence.
map sharks
Is America's credit crisis inflicting on America's credit card market?
If there's blood in the water, it's only natural for dorsal fins to swirl arousingly.
Now that America's real estate market is ailing, predators are targeting the country's credit card market.
Analysts at Goldman Sachs reckon that credit card losses could reach 99 billion dollars if the risk of contagion of underfunded loans spreads to other forms of consumer credit.
Signs of tension are clearly visible.
There are increases both in terms of debits and default rates that measure the proportion of loans that are unretractable or more than 30 days late.
HSBC announced last month that it has taken a 1.4 billion-dollar fee collection out of its American consumer funding, partly because of the weakness of credit card borrowers.
Nevertheless, it is too early to panic.
Losses and faces are still low.
According to rating agency Moody, the third-quarter default rate of 3.89% was almost a full percentage point below the historical average.
Deterioration at rates can be explained in part by technical factors.
An amendment to U.S. private bankruptcy law in 2005 resulted in an abrupt case of bankruptcy filings, which in turn account for a large part of theirt card losses; the number of applications (and thus default fees) would rise again as to whether or not deteriorate general conditions for borrowers.
The industry is also reporting solid payment rates showing how much of its debt consumers pay off each month
and confidence in credit card-based securities is quite strong, despite the standstill in other corners of structured financing.
Dennis Moroney of TowerGroup, a research firm, expects that spending volumes for 2007 will ultimately be 25% higher than last year.
There are certainly direct channels of infection between the real estate credit crisis and the credit card market: it is more likely that consumers will take out credit card debt, now that home ownership loans are drying up.
However, the card issuers look at the flow of money instead of capital values, which is why falling house prices do not necessarily trigger a change in the creditworthiness of the borrower.
They can even be beneficial for the card issuers.
Incentives for consumers to continue to pay mortgage installments decrease if the properties are worth less than the value of the loan; as a result, the card debt among repayment priorities becomes more important.
Card issuers can also react much faster and more flexibly than lenders to the more stormy terms by changing interest rates or loan limits.
This should theoretically reduce the risk of a rapid revaluation of assets.
"We'ren't going to wake up one day and completely re-evaluate the loans," says Gary Perlin, Capital One's main financial adviser.
Even if a sudden undercover crisis in the credit card market is unlikely, the risks of a sustained downturn are considerably more real.
If lower home prices and a credit reduction push America into recession, the industry will undoubtedly face a grimmer future.
Keep an eye out for these shark fins.
British benchers plead guilty in Enron case
Three British bankers who were due to stand trial for their role in the fraudulent system with Enron's former chief financial manager, Andrew Fastow, pleaded guilty on Wednesday.
David Bermingham, Giles Darby and Gary Mulgrew had originally pleaded innocent for seven counts of fraud using telecommunications for allegedly conspiracing Fastow in a secret financial scam in 2000 to enrich themselves at the expense of their employer.
Proceedings had been scheduled for January.
But during a hearing in chart before U.S. District Judge Ewing Werlein Junior, everyone pleaded guilty for a case of fraudulence using telecommunications.
Greenwich NatWest's three former executives, a unit of Royal Bank of Scotland Group PLC, have become known in Britain over the course of extradition proceedings that lasted two years.
In the United States, her case is a loose end to an investigation launched after Enron's 2001 collapse.
The accusations originally filed against them in 2002 made Fastow aware that he was the aim of an investigation by the government related to Enron decline.
The indictment against the bankers - dubbed the "NatWest Three" - claims they came to Houston in 2000 to concoct a fraudulent plot with Fastow and his former top adviser Michael Kopper.
Greenwich Natwest had invested in a subsidiary of an Enron partnership controlled by Fastow, who was the architect of countless fraudulent Enron machinations that helped tear the firm into the bankruptcy vortex.
At the beginning of 2002, the bank had estimated the value of its stake in the subsidiary at zero, but the three British men knew that it actually had significant value.
A company under Kopper's scrutiny acquired the bank's shares in the subsidiary for 1 million dollars.
The bankers paid Kopper 250,000 dollars for a stake in that company.
According to the indictment, Fastow mistakenly represented to Enron that the energy company would pay 20 million dollars to Greenwich NatWest for their shares in the subsidiary.
But in fact, the 20 million dollars went to Britain's bankers, Fastow and others.
The bankers received 7.3 million dollars, while Fastow, Kopper and others discouraged about 12.3 million dollars, according to the indictment.
In January 2004, Fastow pleaded guilty on two counts of conspiracy for his role in Enron's bankruptcy.
The British trio were arrested three months later.
Fastow is serving a 6-year prison sentence in the federal prison of Louisiana.
Kopper sits a sentence of three years and one month at a facility in Texarkana.
Each of the three men were on the loose due to 1 million dollars in bail, which required them to live in the United States in anticipation of legal proceedings.
Enron, once the nation's seventh-largest company, crumbled in bankruption in December 2001, after years of accounting tricks could no longer obscure billion-dollar debts or make weavering ventures seem profitable.
The collapse exterminated thousands of jobs, more than 60 billion market value and more than two-billion-dollar pension plans.
Enron founder Kenneth Lay and former CEO Jeffrey Skilling were convicted last year for their roles related to Enron collapse.
Skilling serves a sentence of more than 24 years.
Lay's convictions of conspiracy, fraud and other charges became void after passing since he died last year for heart disease.
Not at the bottom of Moscow
The price of a Gulfstream G550 aircraft, equipped with a Rolls-Royce engine and enough range to fly non-stop from Moscow to Los Angeles, is at least 54 million dollars.
But that's change for some of the rich Russians.
"It accommodates up to 18 passengers," said Dan Firer, a Gulfstream representative.
"But none of you are asking for so many seats because it's for a private family or a corporate president."
Firer was at the Gulfstream booth last Friday at the millionaire exhibition, a four-day orgy of flaunted consumption held in a convoluted exhibition center on the outskirts of the capital.
In addition to Gulfstream, more than 200 other luxury providers had built exhibition booths, hoping to interest newly-cashed Russians in everything from mahogany-paneled yachts to diamond-covered carries that would be appropriate for 50 cents.
Firer cautiously targeted a glossy orange Porsche at the closest booth.
"That's a nice car, but it's change compared to us," he said.
"After the United States, Russia is the second-largest market for private aircraft."
Fueled by an oil boom, Russia's capital seems as flooded with money as Dallas during its heydays.
"We went through the same phase in the 1890s, when some Americans came to gigantic wealth and wentered to Europe in droves, where they were seen as materialistic," said Marshall Goldman, a scientist at the Davis Center for Russian and Eurasian Studies at Harvard University.
Like the Saudi-Arab overachievers of the 1970s and the Japanese in the 1990s, the Russians emerge as the most visible in the world's most visibly free-live money issuers.
The most exclusive luxury brands are looking for their share of the
Elsewhere in Moscow this week, a conference of the luxury industry attracted Donatella Versace, Tom Ford and Bernard Arnault, the chairman of LVMH, to exchange ideas on how to sell high-priced goods in markets such as Russia.
(The conference is organized by The International Herald Tribune, which belongs to the New York Times Company)
Russia has 53 billionaires who own a total of 282 billion dollars, according to the Forbes list of the richest people.
In addition, 103,000 Russian millionaires belong to the second class, which together is worth 670 billion dollars, according to a study on the development of private assets in the former Soviet Union, written by economic experts from the Center for Economic and Financial Research, an expert panel here.
(The World Bank says 15.8 percent of Russians lived below subsistence in 2005.)
"There are hundreds of millionaires in Moscow - that's where we're all here," said Nicole Marais, a sales and marketing representative of David Morris, a London jeweler.
She had visited the fair for the first time together with the brand's Russian retailer, the Soho Jewelry Group.
"Women are all exemplary and the men are all worth a fortune."
As they move through the halls Sunday night in evening clothes, two real estate agents, Evgenia Yumatova, 21, and Svetlana Turovtsova, 26, refer to themselves and their clients as millionaires.
"I've been looking for the cars and I'm trying to choose between the Infiniti, the Mercedes and the Porsche," Ms Yumatova, who delivered the designer clothes she wore, was like a red carpet veteran with Joan Rivers.
The traffic fair seemed primarily aimed at the new riches, the type of brand-conscious Russian buyers who would be willing to stand for several hours in a traffic jam to visit a drafty warehouse that simplistically set Cadillac Escalades, pocket knives made of platinum, Mövenpick Eiskrem, tailor-made billiard tables, apartments in Dubai and Gund teddy bears next to each other.
The public was able to buy tickets for 30 euros (44.57 dollars).
"The Russians are keen to learn which brand is the most expensive and best," said Mr Gijrath, who sat patriotically at a trade fair stall of Dutch porcelain manufacturer Royal Delft in front of a wall-sized imitation of a Rembrandt image of hand-painted, blue mosaic stones.
"Of course, Russians are more interested in Klimper-Klimper."
In fact, the traders here say that the Russians with old money (10 to 15 years old, according to Russian standards) have already moved from large-scale logos to the kind of sober brands with limited quantities that wouldn’t market themselves at a trading show.
Nevertheless, several thousand people, whether millionaires, envoys of millionaires or simply tourists pushed through the halls every day.
And some of them bought.
On the first night of the show last Thursday, Roman Ershov, the general manager of King Koil Betten here, said he had sold two 70,000 dollar mattress sets made of silk, cashmere and cotton, with sewn-in gold threads.
Also on the first night, Mikhail Lyubiumov, a sales manager for A1 Moto, told a company equipped the high-end cars with high-end alarm systems that his company had sold the glaring orange Porsche for 170,000 dollars to a Russian millionaire.
By Sunday afternoon, Andrei Karapetian, the president of L.A. Connection, a company that is adapting luxury cars to customer requirements, said he would have sold three car radiator grilles for a Rolls-Royce Phantom, the interlinked chain bracelets, for 55,000 dollars each.
He showed a reporter a car tire developed for a Russian casino owner, with spokes he had coated with colored Swarovski crystals, so it looked like a roulette wheel.
The cost: 22,000 dollars for a four-breather.
"The Russian millionaire, who wants ostric leather wheels or a picture of his wife's face from cubic zirconium, won't necessarily ride on those wheels," Mr Karapetian said.
"He'll keep it in his 30- or 40-car garage."
Even if many of his Russian customers are rich, Mr Karapetian said the phrase "Russian millionaires" wouldn't express much exclusivity at the moment, given the rise in local property prices.
"If you bought an apartment in Moscow three or four years ago, they're presumably already a millionaire, at least on paper, if you'ren't in cash," he said.
"There are more of those than you think."
Those who weren't millionaires could, at least, pretend they're rich at a stand operated by Alliance Continental, a Russian wealth management firm.
The company had set up four red phone booths based on the British model, which were crammed with mountains of false million-dollar bills.
Azad Karryev, the company's marketing director, said its employees printed out 500,000 false banknotes for the millionaires fair.
Passers-by paused to throw around the piles of money, cellphone photos as they sit back in the midst of suspending wealth, snapped by friends, or just to pocket a few millions.
"See, in Moscow, everyone can feel like a millionaire," Mr Karryev said.
Botched operation suspected in New Jersey fatality
It was the day before Thanksgiving when Ronald Livingston looked through the large glass window of the Just a Little Healthier restaurant here and watched as an ambulance stopped two doors further down West Scott Avenue at the white Cape Cod.
Soon afterwards there was a police car there.
And then there was another.
"Then I seen the box truck of the medical coroner come close," Livingston, a supervisor of the restaurant, said Wednesday.
"That was when I knew someone had died."
The authorities learned that the dead woman was Olga Arroyo, 41, an assistant to the director of the Newark Police Department.
Now investigators are trying to determine if she died because of a procedure carried out by an unauthorised cosmetic surgeon whom the police did not identify and who worked in the home on West Scott Avenue 212.
Authorities have not yet determined what caused Ms Arroyo's death, and are hopeful that toxicological investigations, the results of which may not be available for weeks, will help enlighten a mysterious death that has sented mourning through the leadership of the Newark police.
Ms Arroyo had been an assistant to the Police Director, Garry F. McCarthy, since September 2006, and her voice was known to many in the department.
"She was loyal, hard-working and stubborn," Mr McCarthy said about Ms. Arroyo in a telephone interview Wednesday.
“She was my pit bull.
She protected me.”
McCarthy said Ms Arroyo was the wife of Jimmy Arroyo, a former Newark police officer who works for the alcohol control commission, and that she is leaving two sons, 14 and 11, and a stepdaughter.
Eileen Walsh, a spokeswoman for Union County prosecutor Theodore J Romankov, said investigators would try to determine whether Arroyo died as a result of unapproved surgical treatment.
"That's one of the possibilities," Ms Walsh said.
“It’s an ongoing investigation.”
Authorities said they had arrived at the house at about 3 a.m. in November.
And they found Ms Arroyo on a couch.
She was pronounced dead at the scene.
More than a week has passed and the authorities have communicated little about their investigation or about the owner of the house, which is listed as Flor Margarita Castro.
Walsh didn't want to say if Ms Castro was questioned by authorities.
Investigators are trying to determine whether part of the home was used as a medical practice.
On Wednesday, no one opened the door to Ms. Castro's house, where a wilted rose shrub stands in the skimpy front garden and a Dalmatian barks behind the house.
A blue barrel labeled "Ethyl Vanillin" - used as a substitute for vanilla in food and perfumes - sat in the parking lot beside a door leading to the basement.
Two mountain bikes hung by hook on one side.
McCarthy said that when he first arrived in Newark after spending 25 years with the New York Police Department, Ms Arroyo was invaluable, helping him navigate the provincial and hard political culture in his new city.
"She said, 'You have to pay attention to this or that,'" he said.
"She's always been here when I got here and she was always here when I left.
It was embarrassing."
Hopes that the central bank will cut interest rates lead to the rise in world stock markets
Shares jumped on both sides of the Atlantic yesterday after market hopes that the central bank will try to reassure credit markets through further interest rate cuts have been reinforced by appeasing comments from the U.S
Stock markets rose in London, New York and across Europe after Donald Kahn, the number 2 of Ben Bernanke, the central bank's chairman, said the policy had to respond "flexibly and pragmatically" to continued pressure on financial markets.
Reassuring angrieous markets in a clear effort in the face of signs of emerging credit tensions, Kohn said recent turbulence had "partially reversed some of the improvements in market functions from the late part of September and October."
Should the increased turbulence persist, it would increase the possibility of further tightening of financial conditions for consumers and businesses, he said.
The comments have been picked up by investors as a signal that the central bank is prepared to step in and stabilise credit conditions with new capital injections into U.S. markets and possible further interest rate cuts.
The remarks also helped reassure Wall Street in light of further bad news from the U.S. economy, now that U.S. real estate sales reached a new record low in October.
The consequences of financial turbulence were underlined by Bear Stearn's announcement, the investment bank, that it would eliminate 650 jobs, or 4 percent of its global workers, in a cost-reducing measure to make up for heavy losses from their lost bets in the U.S. mortgage lending sector.
Only 20 of these jobs are expected to disappear in London, where the bank employs 1,500 people.
In New York, the Dow Jones industrial index jumped 331 points, or 2.55 percent, to close at 13,289.40, while the broader S&P 500 index rose 2.65 percent.
In London, the FTSE 100 index rose 165.5 points, or 2.7 per cent, to close at 6,306.2, adding more than 39 billion pounds to the value of UK defaults.
Germany's Dax index rose 2.6 percent and France's CAC40 rose 2.3 percent.
Earlier, the resurgence of credit pressure on the markets had been underlined after strong demand for an auction of three-month guarantees from the European Central Bank granted to the European Commercial Banks been lentered at a considerable 4.7 per century, the highest rate since April 2001.
