England confronted with decision over Owais Shah and Ravi Bopara
Here two dilemmas, each almost as difficult as the other.
Who's supposed to be number 6 for England in Kandy this weekend, Owais Shah or Ravi Bopara?
And, no less relevant in a broader context than the first test against Sri Lanka, who should make the decision?
The second question is important because David Graveney, the electoral chairman, on appeal at least under this particular title, is here to help the head coach Peter Moores and captain Michael Vaughan in doing so, and because the table of High Morries, the English managing director, gradually fills with applications for national electoral offices, if not for those part-time.
Whoever doesn't get the sixth racket position will be unhappy, but life is often unjust and the sport is undoubtedly.
It's never easy to nail down exactly what allows a person to reach the top of his profession, while someone else will never get beyond the lowest rank, sometimes for lack of happiness rather than ability.
It's not just in cricket, where it's difficult to choose, where judgments are needed that are always subjective in the end.
Is it the right time for Bopara to rise?
Is it fair for Shah if he does?
The same can perhaps also be asked in another area, such as John Edwards or "Tubby" Banerjee, bot of both intelligent young executives with good degrees who know the business well should be promoted.
Who can say what's the right class for a cricketer, unless he's given the opportunity to swim or perish.
Of course, numbers have a great influence on decisions, but chances also have this.
Within a team, a cricket player has to catch the captain's eye at the right time if he can take a wicket and a racket has to get regular chances in order if he wants to make an impression as a one-day cricker.
Catching the eyes of the selectors is more complicated.
There were many who were better than last season, for example, as James Anderson, who will apparently play in the first Test, even if Stephen Harmison declares himself healthy and how the wind bones into the nets.
Anderson took 32 first-class wickets with 33 runs each to earn a position in this tour.
Chris Tremlett took only 29 with the same modest average, but both had their highlights and are hoping for even more.
Whether Shah or Bopara plays at Asgiriya Stadium can only be a question of view.
Choosing could decide both men's careers, or it might make little difference after all.
Four years ago in Sri Lanka Gareth Batty played in every Test and was successful in the first, with five wickets and 40 useful runs, but it was Paul Collingwood, at that time still struggling to consolidate, who made the breakthrough into a significant Test cricket player and who, despite scoring a double-hundred against Australia in Australia, is perhaps still underestimated.
It is likely that Graveney, Vaughan and Moores, after another discussion in the wake of today's net session at Asgiriya Stadium, will follow their conservative instincts and gully sense for Shah's greater experience about Bopara's youthful enthusiasm.
There are arguments for both of them.
For Shah, his sparkling and valuable 88 test-debut innings in Bombay speaks, 43 top-notch hundreds and just six from Bopara, who is seven-years younger at 22, and an excellent record against twisted bullets.
But Shah made only ten runs in his two innings in the May against West Indies in the test in Lord, his home arena as a player from middlesex, and this one, plus his immediate rejection from the selectors as Vaughan was healthy to take back his position for the next game, appears to have taken the momentum out of his beating, which was still evident in Bombay.
In contrast, the all-rounder from Essex has shoveling joie de vivre.
He isn't impressed by anything on a cricket pitch and his stance away from that was put in a nutshell when he recently overturned a four-wheel-drive vehicle in an exhibition for England's sponsor Volkswagen.
He suffered minor injuries and presumably a shock, but the first thing he did was to get out his Vodafone (also sponsored, of course) and invoke his team-mate Alastair Cook, who had observed this from afar, and to say with enthusiasm: "Did you see that?".
Bopara is a much better fielder, he bowls his medium pace with some effect here on the playing fields, which seems to suit him and beats with confidence and skill.
Choosing the right man is one thing, choosing the right time another.
Maybe that's the real art of choice.
I'm tended to give Bopara his chance now, which doesn't mean Shah mayn't look good after all, if, as Ceylon's tea set suggests, he's the one who's going to play here this weekend.
Buses bring refugees back to work - and to fight al-Qaeda
The first busloads of Iraqi refugees from Syria arrived in Baghdad yesterday, with a stream of distributors whose funds or visas have expired, and others who hoped the city will return to normality.
Some had even come back to fight the rebels they had expelled from their homes.
At Mansour bus station, even before the arrival of 800 people from Damascus in an official government convoy of a dozen buses, the terminal was busy with refugees from Syria and Jordan who had returned to private transports.
"I decided to go to Syria after killing a lot of doctors in Baghdad," Sabah Al-Qubaisi, a Sunni doctor, said.
"I came back today because of what I've seen on TV and what I've read in the newspaper about what happened in Baghdad, and how it would be safe now."
They say that the Mahdi army has stopped killing people," he said, referring to the Shiite militias of militant clericist Moqtada Al-Sadr, who has ordered his powers to return at this time.
Qassim al Atwani, a 38-year-old Shiite teacher from the predominant Sunni part of Amariyah, returned with his two children because he was unable to renewer his Syrian visa and because the Iraqi regime is not allowed to work.
His Shiite neighbors in Amariyah called him to tell him that a local U.S.-backed militia has helped expell Al-Qaeda from their bastion in the west of the capita, and urged him to return.
One of the newcomers from Jordan was the leader of a Sunni and Shiite tribe who had been driven out of his home in Sleikh in north Baghdad by an attack with hand grenades after urging his fellow citizens to fight Al-Qaidas.
He had come back to fight.
"Two days ago, some of my friends called me and told me that they started one the anti-al-Qaeda militia in Sleikh and that they needed my support and those of my boys.
That's why I came back today," said Sabaah Alu Obeidi, 62.
Many said they had been lured by increasing calm in the capital since the U.S. troople increase and the growth of "group-affected citizens" who were armed and defending their neighborhoods in cooperation with the American military.
But more spoke of having been forced home by visa problems and depleting funds in Damascus, where more than 1.3 million Iraqis had escaped in recent years.
Salaam al-Samarrai, a 51-year-old three-year-old father, expressed general suspicion among exiles that Nouri al-Maliki, the Shiite prime minister, had asked the Syrian government to impose visa restrictions on the refugees to force people to return.
More than two and a half million people are believed to have fled the sectarian death squadrons in the country.
"We were doing well until al-Maliki visited Syria and the government was no longer urging the Iraqis to give visas to force them to return to Iraq," the Sunni pensioner said.
“I can’t trust a government that supports the criminals who burned down my house after I walked away.”
Part of the reason for the decline in violence is that Sunnis have been expelled from Shiite residential areas and vice versa, leading to the creation of many places where tensions have subsided.
There are fears that a sudden influx of refugees will stir up sectarian tensions again and there will be conflicts.
Small Saarland, good education
The smallest federal state is a leader in vocational training and shines even with the international orientation of its universities.
Only in a field of education does the Saar countries push the same deficit as the rest of the country.
"I explained yesterday for the 50th time to a non-Saarlander that we are not French" - the smallest federal state self-deprecating on his 50th birthday placates this slogan.
France and Luxembourg play a major role in the Saarland’s education system, which ranks fiffth in the education monitor of the Institute of German Economics Cologne (IW).
Thus, the universities cooperate closely with the neighboring countries out of tradition.
The university in Saarbrücken was founded in 1948 by the French.
To this day, students can complete their studies here with a double or even triple diploma.
Accordingly, the proportion of foreign students is high at 15.6 percent.
But even the national children bring the Saarland in above-average numbers to the universities - many of them without a graduation at the high school.
"In Saarland you use the vocational training system," says Axel Plünnecke, an education expert at the IW.
More than one in five young people acquire their eligibility to study at a vocational school, so the Saarland is more than seven percentage points above the federal average.
The combination possibilities of vocational and academic training are also comparatively strongly developed.
Compared to the population, the equipment with dual degree programs is the second best in Germany.
However, despite practical training and increasing demand, the number of engineering graduates has fallen by a third in recent years.
Therefore, the Saarland Ministry of Education has now committed the universities to a marketing campaign for engineering subjects.
"We initiated a number of reforms at the general education schools after the first Pisa study," says a spokeswoman for the Ministry of Education.
That's how the government introduced nationwide comparative tests.
In order for the children to start learning earlier, the last day-care year is free of charge - a model that other federal states are now also copying.
Whether the achievements of the students improve sustainably will only be shown in future comparative studies.
At Pisa in 2003, the proportion of high-risk pupils shrank from 29 to 20 per cent.
Overall, however, the Saarland did not increase as much as other federal states.
The Bavarians calculate better.
And to the annoyance of the Saar countries, foreign language skills are not measured at Pisa - in this area one could otherwise score points.
Because the language of the neighbors is obligatory at grammar schools - even if the Saarlanders are not
Chávez converts watches
In Venezuela, the clocks will soon tick differently than in the rest of the world.
On December 9, President Hugo Chávez will turn back the timepieces in the South American country by half an hour.
In Spain, meanwhile, Chávez becomes a joking figure.
According to a new law, according to a new law, the clocks will be set at world time of minus four and a half hours.
Thus, the South American state gets a time zone that does not exist in any other country in the world.
U.S. hostile Chávez justified his decision, among other things, by saying that the United States had imposed the division into time zones at full hours.
Chávez also announced on Wednesday (local time) because of a hostage affair the termination of relations with neighboring Colombia announced.
The left-wing nationalist said that as long as conservative Alvaro Uribe remains president of Colombia, he will not maintain relations either as head of state nor the Colombian government.
"I can't do this simply because of my dignity," said Chávez, who is pushing ahead with a constitutional reform on which the citizens of the oil country must vote in a referendum on Sunday.
With the reform, he would significantly expand his power in the country.
The dispute prompted Uribe's decision to strip Chávez of the mandate for negotiations with Marxist rebels in Colombia, which involved the release of hostages.
Uribe "spit" Venezuela in the face with his decision, Chávez had criticized on Sunday.
Chávez is on everyone's lips in Spain, too.
A new mobile phone ringing sound causes a sensation in the country: "Por qué no te callas" (Why don't you keep your mouth) is the sentence that more than half a million people have already downloaded from the Internet.
Spoken him Spain's King Juan Carlos, who was annoyed at the recent Ibero-America summit by the left-wing populist's constant interludes and had crossed his mouth.
Germans threaten 2007 wage minus
Despite the strong economy, German workers are threatening a real wage loss this year, according to a forecast.
Experts cited a whole bundle of reasons for their pessimistic prognosis.
According to a calculation by the Hans-Böckler-Foundation, the employees could expect less on the salary account in real terms in 2007 than in the previous year.
"This is due to the modest wage development, the increase in VAT and the price increases in food and energy," Gustav Horn of the Institute for Macroeconomics and Business Cycle Research told the Bild newspaper.
New burdens could also come to workers when it comes to unemployment insurance.
Finance Minister Peer Steinbrück told the "Neue Osnabrücker Zeitung" that the planned reduction of unemployment insurance to 3.3 percent at the turn of the year was indeed correct and reasonable in the current situation.
However, the Federal Employment Agency should not be embarrassed to have to ask the federal government for loans or grants, the SPD politician was quoted as saying.
On Tuesday it was announced that the price explosion in food and petroleum products drove the rate of inflation to the highest level in 13 years.
Overall prices rose 3.0 percent year-on-on-year.
Veneus is Earth's hellish twin
In spite of its hostile atmosphere on earth, Venus is more similar than previously thought: The European spacecraft "Venus Express" has discovered lightning in the atmosphere and wind vortices at the poles, as they also occur on earth in winter.
For nearly three decades, scientists have suspected it's flashing on Venus.
Now the European probe "Venus Express" has provided proof - she tracked down electromagnetic waves on the edge of the atmosphere: The lightning bolts on Venus shoot from cloud to cloud at an altitude of around 56 kilometers, reports Christopher Russel of the University of California in Los Angeles in the science magazine "Nature."
According to the famous "primordial soup theory", lightning has created life on Earth: thunderstorms are said to have stimulated reactions in the ocean, which led to the formation of amino acids, the building blocks of life.
"If there was ever life there, it must have been in very early history," explains Sean Solomon of the Carnegie Institution in Washington.
Today there should be hardly anything alive on the surface of Venus: The atmosphere is around a hundred times denser than on Earth, there are sometimes temperatures of 500 degrees.
"Venus may be Earth's devil twin, but it's a twin," Russel said.
Why Venus, which is so similar to Earth in mass and size, has embarked on such a completely different development, is the basic question of the Esa mission.
The launch of "Venus Express" in November 2005 was a milestone for Venus research, as there hasn't been a probe to explore this planet since the early 1990s.
According to the researchers, the latest research, which will now be presented in several "Nature" articles, not only underscores the uniqueness of Venus within the solar system.
"They also show that the similarities of our neighboring planet with the Earth are greater than initially assumed," Esa announces.
In addition to the already known whirlwind at the north pole, the researchers also found a swirl of clouds at the south pole, which rotates even faster.
The phenomenon is reminiscent of airflows in the earthly winter hemisphere, writes a team of researchers led by Giuseppe Piccioni of the Italian Istituto di Astrofisica Spaziale e Fisica Cosmica.
The solar system's second-insinest planet has almost the same mass as the Earth and, with a diameter of 12,100 kilometers, is almost as large.
The planet, also known as the morning or evening star, orbits around the sun in just under 225 days.
However, it revolves much slower around itself: a day of Venus lasts 243 earth days.
Although Venus with surface temperatures of 460 degrees Celsius and a pressure of 92 bar is hardly reminiscent of the Earth, scientists assume that both planets were formed under very similar conditions.
On both, water accumulated, which remained on Earth in oceans but was lost into space on Venus.
And while carbon dioxide is fixed on Earth in carbonate rocks, on Venus it accounts for the majority of the atmosphere.
Köhler defends Merkel's meeting with Dalai Lama
Federal President Köhler demands an end to the party dispute over the reception of the Dalai Lama in the chancellery.
He indirectly criticized the SPD because of their attacks on the chancellor after the meeting in September.
Federal President Horst Köhler is concerned about Germany's reputation and has called on the grand coalition to end the dispute over the reception of the Dalai Lama.
"We shouldn't present a divided nation to the outside world on this issue," Köhler told Handelsblatt.
At the same time, Köhler signaled understanding for the Dalai Lama’s reception by Chancellor Angela Merkel (CDU) in September.
"Human rights have universal validity for me, even if I have respect for the diversity of cultures," Köhler said.
Everyone knows that Germany must maintain sensitive balances in foreign policy, emphasized the Federal President, but took Merkel from the accusation that she pursues a new China policy.
"The chancellor of the exchequer has not refrained from the traditional one-China policy.
China also knows that," Köhler said.
Foreign Minister Frank-Walter Steinmeier (SPD) had indirectly expressed displeasure about the reception of the Dalai Lama and had therefore been attacked from the ranks of the Union.
Steinmeier had lamented, among other things, the "shop window policy" of the Union in foreign policy.
After the reception of the Dalai Lamas in the Chancellery, there was intense irritation in the German-Chinese relationship.
For example, the Chinese leadership has not only uninvited German Finance Minister Peer Steinbrück (SPD), but, according to SPIEGEL, also frozen the so-called strategic dialogue of the foreign ministries of both countries.
China's Prime Minister Wen Jiabao has now urged German Chancellor Angela Merkel to concede the Dalai Lama's reception as a mistake.
China rejects it when European politicians, in an official capacity, receive the religious leader of Tibetans, Wen Jiabao reaffirmed the Chinese position.
The actions and words of the Dalai Lamas showed that he was trying to secede Tibet and the wider Tibetan region, which also includes other present-day provinces, from China.
Schäuble warns of riots as in France
Street battles with the police, gunfire, burning cars: Wolfgang Schäuble warns of riots as in France.
Everything must be done so that social dissatisfaction in Germany does not discharge into violence on the streets, demanded the Federal Minister of the Interior.
Barricades burned, rioters shot at the police with shotguns: After the serious youth riots in France, German Interior Minister Wolfgang Schäuble (CDU) fears a similar development in Germany.
"We have to make sure that social explosives do not build up and discharge into violence.
We have to take this seriously," he told the "Neue Presse" published in Hanover.
Not every young person can cope with the rapid change in social reality.
The federal government, states and local authorities were already working vigorously to reduce problems, Schäuble said.
This isn’t just about the integration of migrants.
"From the integration summit to the Islam conference, this already shows first successes."
However, it is too early for all-clear.
Schäuble also pointed out that there are non-urban and sociological problems in German suburbs, such as in France.
In the northern suburbs of Paris, teenagers had fought heavy street battles with the police in the past few nights.
Cars and public buildings were also burning in Toulouse.
The chairman of the Turkish community in Germany demanded greater integration efforts of the German government, Kenan Kolat.
Faced with the riots in France, Kolat warned of "imitation effects."
The situation in Germany is not as explosive as in France.
However, there is still a need for action.
Kolat pointed out that the unemployment rate among adolescents with a migrant background under the age of 25 is "above average."
Above all, it is necessary to provide for more apprenticeships.
Kolat emphasized: "We have to give young people a perspective."
However, the chairman of the Turkish community added that "societal scrutiny is taking place in families" in Germany more than in France.
In addition, many young people with a migrant background did not want to destroy what their parents have built here.
Kolat said, "But we still have to pay attention."
"We need to give young people the opportunity to participate in work and training."
Meanwhile, it remained largely quiet during the night in the Paris suburb of Villiers-le-Bel.
Hundreds of police were in use, a helicopter flew over the area with headlights.
Competent authorities reported three arrests as well as a few burning bins and cars.
Since the day before, a squad of around 1000 security forces has ensured calm.
The police will crack down as long as it is necessary, announced Interior Minister Michelle Alliot-Marie after a visit to the suburban.
At night on Tuesday, around 80 police officers were injured in riots in Villiers-le-Bel.
As a result, unrest spread to Toulouse.
The riots ignited at a traffic accident in which two youths had died on Sunday.
