In the realm of Angela Wolke
The chancellor's unrealistic appearance documents that the coalition is at an end in terms of content - and that nothing is to be expected from herself anymore.
In which country does this chancellor actually live?
After her bleak appearance in the general debate of the Bundestag, which was fussy and low in content, one has to say: not here - not in the country that would finally have to be ruled by her.
The accusation that she focuses too much on foreign policy, Angela Merkel has rejected with a very overbearing sentence.
This is provincial, she said, showing that she does not want to comprehend what the critics are about.
In the Bundestag, she has recognizably documented for everyone how much their accusation applies.
There was neither concrete interest nor even a feeling to feel for the problems in the country.
Instead, Angela Wolke presented herself: a detached politician who flees into foreign policy and even stays there in the rough.
Example of climate protection: She praises herself unabashed for the decisions of Heiligendamm, but does not say how the climate goals should be achieved in Germany.
In general, self-praise - so much immodestity the CDU leader would have failed two years ago.
Now she clings to the floskel that the upsurge arrives among the people.
In doing so, she is damaging her image as a pragmatist, which solves problems instead of talking the world beautifully.
But in the realm of Angela Wolke, domestic political problems are a marginal phenomenon.
Child poverty and the concerns of low-income earners?
She's got a few sub-sentences left for it.
It is characteristic of how often she expects when it comes to the child surcharge.
Merkel needs three attempts there to bring out a halfway straight sentence.
She stumbles like on foreign territory when it becomes concrete.
Attack department
In the YouTube debate of the Republican presidential candidates, the two favorites Rudy Giuliani and Mitt Romney deliver themselves a hard exchange of blows - and must now fear an outsider candidate.
The tension of the candidates is already unloaded on the first topic.
He has made New York a "place of refuge for illegal immigrants," Mitt Romney accuses his opponent Rudy Giuliani.
"I'd say his own house was a haven for illegals," Giuliani replies, accusing the former governor of Massachusetts of having privately employed undocumented undocumented immigrants.
The nominees are blowing to the attack: Because the Republican base continues to have no clear favorite, the eight presidential contenders use the debate on CNN mainly to discredit their adversaries.
The self-shot 30-second videos that Internet users sent in via YouTube are little more than keyword providers.
"With this kind of isolationism, we've left Hitler to power," John McCain accuses his opponent, Ron Paul, who is the only one in the round calling for an immediate withdrawal from Iraq.
Romney, too, gets the Levites vigorously readed by the Vietnam veteran when he refuses to convict the so-called "waterboarding," an interrogation method in which drowning is simulsified as torture.
That the important topics of the campaign, however, are now beyond Iraq war and torture debates, shows the reactions of the hall audience in St. Petersburg in Florida.
When conservative values are praised and future tax increases are rejected, applause burns up in the auditorium.
This shows once again where the weaknesses of the two favorites lie.
"I was wrong at the time," Romney declares contritely, in front of grassroots and televised audience for the fact that he was still one of the abortion advocates in 1994.
Giuliani, on the other hand, swerves when he is asked for his opinion on the literal interpretation of the Bible.
Musharraf rules in civilian for the first time
A day after his resignation as army chief, the Pakistani ruler Musharraf has been sworn in as president.
Eight-year military rule ends in Pakistan.
Pakistan's President Pervez Musharraf was sworn in for another five-year term on Thursday.
Pakistan television broadcast the ceremony live at the presidential palace of Islamabad.
Musharraf vowed to respect the Constitution and act for the benefit of the nation.
On Wednesday, he handed over his office as army chief to his confidant Ashfaq Kiyani and thus bowed to international pressure, especially from the USA.
Eight years after his seizure of power in a bloodless military coup, the former general now rules for the first time as civilian head of state.
With his resignation as Commander-in-Chief of the Armed Forces, Musharraf had met a core demand from the opposition and the West.
Another central demand is an end to the state of emergency imposed by Musharraf on November 3.
Soon after his swearing-in, Musharraf will, according to Attorney General Malik Muhammad Qayyum, lift the state of emergency and thus meet another demand from the West.
The country's Supreme Court, which Musharraf had occupied after imposing a state-of-inauguration in early November weighed lawyers, last week dismissed all appeals against the controversial re-election of the head of state on October 6.
Meanwhile, five soldiers were killed and four others wounded in the northern tribal region of northern Waziristan in an attack on a military convoy.
According to an army spokesman, the bomb exploded on a road near the border with Afghanistan.
The fathers of Krautrock return
Germany's supergroup of the seventies makes music again: The three band members of Harmonia performed their consoles again at a concert in Berlin.
For the revival, a recording from her big time "Live 1974" appears.
Even then, they were much more famous abroad.
The arranged sensation then occurs in the form of three set gentlemen on the evening table.
Everyone has their box in front of them, their cables and, above all, their buttons.
Dieter Moebius towers in the blossom-white shirt at the age of 63 in the middle.
On the left flanked by Michael Rother, 57 years young and in possession of laptop and guitar.
On the right, viewed from the auditorium, Hans-Joachim Roedelius, 73, curves concentrated on the console.
Everyone contributes noises, sometimes even a beat.
And sometimes it accidentally blends into a conventional piece of music.
That doesn't seem to bother the gentlemen themselves much either.
The fact that the band Harmonia makes music again has caused some excitement among Krautrock lovers.
While one may reveal here: Targeted inquiries showed that although the name buzzed through collective pop memory, it was by no means sustainable in order to miss this band painfully for 32 years.
But why shouldn’t the German be allowed to celebrate his living legends from the seventies?
Especially since what misleadingly called Krautrock was always more true in England and America than in his own country.
When the music industry awarded the survivors of the Cologne group Can the Echo Prize for life's work in 2003, one looked into questioning German pop faces in the hall.
When the Red Hot Chili Peppers also thanked the bands La Düsseldorf and Neu! in the laudation, the astonishment knew no limits.
At concerts, Michael Rother later squeaked with the Red Hot Chili Peppers on stage like an old buddy.
The problem of ignorance at home and the transfiguration in a foreign country is one thing.
The memory is overwhelmed with Harmonia because the trio existed for only two years and during this time also unfolded a much lesser power than was subsequently assigned by others.
Moebius and Roedelius had already played with the artist Conrad Schnitzler under the promising name cluster.
At first they played in the basement under the Berlin Schaubühne.
Thereafter in Düsseldorf, for economic reasons for a record label entertained by the church.
Rother had briefly helped out at Kraftwerk and then gained a reputation as a guitarist at Neu!, the most radical German group at the time.
Around 1973, however, Rother followed the cluster musicians into exile to Forst im Weserbergland.
There, in the Altes Weserhof, a ruin from the 16th century, they lived as city-fugitive communards.
Less ideologically than in Commune I, where Roedelius was still allowed to plague himself as a nanny.
It's more in pointy-and-road artist existence.
Supposedly a pennant of the singing club Harmonia Ottenstein was found in bulky waste and a name for art.
Some of this was captured by Harmonia.
The first album was "Music by Harmonia" and the following "De Luxe".
Because so calm and modest the music also sounded, the band suffered material hardship.
Rice collection was not for self-discovery alone.
The now released CD "Live 1974" was recorded at a concert in Griessem, in front of an almost 50-member record backdrop, as someone believes to remember.
At that time, Rother had the desire to promote harmonia and practice occasionally.
An immoral demand for Roedelius and Moebius.
The band declared its separation in 1975.
Rother landed in the esoteric-Spherical and secured his existence with albums such as "Flamming Hearts."
Cluster was revived in the eighties and has been more successful than Harmonia has ever been since.
Herbert Grönemeyer is responsible for Herbert Grönemeyer.
Its record company Grönland aims to nurture Krautrock as its own German contribution to lively music.
New! has already been excavated like Roedelius Solowerk.
The physical resurrection of Harmonia takes place in the Berlin House of Cultures of the world.
Publically subsidised as part of Woldtronics festival.
Julian Cope is there, the Brit, altpunk and author of the manual "Krautrock Sampler."
There's the Loveparade inventor Dr. Motte, too.
With which the entire range of Krautrock's influences on subsequent pop music would be outlined.
Harmonia fills the empty space of the supergroup.
Brian Eno's phrase floats with the strangest sounds in the hall: "The only true music, you're with the future."
There are pictures that show the producer with Harmonia in the farmhouse parlor.
Projections of other black-and-white photos of wired devices are used for logical formation.
Before that, the three veterans are again concerned with improvisation.
No longer by sticking microphones to the organ and on the drums in order to elicit unheared tones from the instruments via self-made effect devices.
Today, electronic music looks like electronic music.
Three tinkerers, which are immersed in technology.
The choir of the house doesn't change that either.
Under the guidance of Barbara Morgenstern is coughed under sound clouds and sung in a beautiful place with emphase "Aaaaaaaah!"
Krautrock has always been a very English term.
It was never rock.
The roots were searched less in blues than in music colleges (can), technologies (powerhouses) or fleeing artistic and social conventions (clusters).
Whoever shouts "'68!" here isn't entirely wrong, and Harmonia won't let that get out of hand even now.
Even the most self-evident is thoroughly reconsidered on the stage.
Where does music start and when does it stop?
This can rob your nerves or give you fleeting moments of great happiness.
Richard Gere isn't cynical enough
"The Hunting Party" and "Murderous Peace" are once again about two films with the Balkan War.
However, both are not convincing.
Either the concepts rely too much on action and bollards or they are too stirring.
Richard Gere can't help in "The Hunting Party" either.
The good news at first: Although the war criminals Karadzic and Mladic are still hiding in the mountains of the Balkans, of all places there, in the gloomy hinterland of the Bosnian war, the actor Richard Gere has now been found again.
We had lost sight of the most beautiful man of the eighties, because too many nice-inconsequential roles in feel-good films for women and too many press conferences on the subject of Buddhism sprouted one's desire to look.
Now Richard Gere is suddenly the most important argument to watch "The Hunting Party - When the Hunter becomes the hunted" (why do film titles today actually always have to be as long and confused as locomotive driver collective agreements?).
He delivers a wonderfully tongue-in-cheek depiction: Basically, his war reporter Sion Hunt is what could have become out of Gere if fate and the film industry were not so gracious: a man who has his very best times behind him for a long time, but who, as a ruinous monument to his demise, has still preserved considerable remnants of the former charm.
But despite this trump feat, "The Hunting Party," along with the german production "Mörderischer Frieden," which also launches this week, continues only the endless series of failed Balkan war films.
"Mörderischer Frieden" can make a short trial: The film is about two German soldiers in Kosovo (Adrian Topol, Max Riemelt) who rescue a Serbin from Kosovarian snipers, whereupon one promptly falls in love with her.
Although it is appreciable that the Serbs aren’t portrayed here as the sole root of all evil, the television-shaft film loses itself all too quickly in bollards and action.
It remains a kind of "alarm for Cobra 11" without a highway but with war for it.
On the other hand, "The Hunting Party" explores why there has actually never been a really good Balkan war film.
The story of three U.S. war reporters set out to track down the Serbian upper war criminal "the fox" might have become a new "Apocalypse Now" with another director and screenwriter (both in Personal Union Richard Shepard) at another time.
Because the stories are surprising: Both times small teams go into the woods to do something that the militaries can’t and both times it’s going crazy.
One of the reasons for the failure of "The Hunting Party" is that you don't just let Simon Hunt be the run-down cynic with heart when he's introduced to us at the beginning.
No, the script has to deny him a romantic affair with a later murdered Muslim Bosnian woman.
"The Hunting Party" finally goes down when, within the last five minutes, the reporters succeed in the time-lapse procedure what Nato, CIA and all bounty hunters in the world have failed in five years: catch the war criminals and leave the Muslim Bosnians for righteous revenge.
This is so bad that one would like to invoke the International Court of Justice in The Hague for crimes against kinologics.
In doing so, the film has a deeply human concern.
He wants to denounce that Karadzic and Mladic are still roaming freely, and he suspects the Americans and the French (here, for once, they should agree) the persecution due to secret political collusions not too intensive.
But well-intentioned and evil are sometimes so similar that a famous Brecht poem comes to mind.
It reads about the wooden mask of an Asian demon on the poet's working table: "Compassionately I see the swollen foreheards, hinting: how exhausting it is to be evil".
Movies like "The Hunting Party" have exactly such cavernous heads, but they hint at how exhausting it is to be good.
The problem isn't limited to the genre of Balkan war film.
Recently it has been reported in this newspaper that all Iraq movies were Hollywood's big flops.
You can add that there has never been a good South Africa movie before.
Almost always applies: When a star drifts around on the crisis herds or minefields of the world, something like "Rangoon" with Patricia Arquette, "Beyond all borders" with Angelina Jolie or "Blood Diamond" with Leonardo DiCaprio.
Perhaps all of the conflicts are just too close in time.
Decades had to pass before Hollywood could make a good film about the Holocaust with "Schindler's List".
Some films will probably still ripen - the now considered classic Vietnam works "The Deer Hunter", "Apocalypse Now" or "Full Metal Jacket" have not been unanimously acclaimed as masterpieces at the time.
But maybe today we know where every mass murder comes to two screens at home in real time, also just too much about the conflicts of the world.
And that robs the screenwriters of the lack of concrent, which it takes to tell good stories.
"Being or nothingness" or "The great dictator" have finally become so good because Lubitsch and Chaplin did not yet know how bad the Nazi regime really prevailed.
Maybe you can't want to be good if you want to make good movies.
Maybe you have to have the sarcastically distanced artist view of the novelist Lawrence Norfolk.
Asked about parallels between his historical novel "Lemprière's Dictionary" and the Balkan War, he replied: "That's just because some things never change.
Big cities will always be terribly dangerous, people will always be assassinated in horrific ways and war will always reign in Yugoslavia."
Von Beust reaps criticism for Olympic cancellation
The mayor's departure from Hamburg's application for the Summer Olympics has met with heavy criticism among politics and business.
Many want to keep fighting for the games on the water.
But von Beust urges realism in assessing the opportunities.
Hans-Jörg Schmidt-Trenz, Managing Director of the Hamburg Chamber of Commerce, on Wednesday described a waiver as "not good for the business location."
No other project in recent times has unfolded such great motivations in the city as the Olympic idea.
"Without this goal, the momentum of sports policy would not be able to be maintained to the same extent."
Schmidt-Trenz called on the Senate not to bury the project, but to find a new long-term approach "that stems from a changed timeline."
Mayor Ole von Beust stayed with previous statements on Wednesday, according to which Hamburg's Olympic ambitions were unrealistic at least until 2028.
"It is not Hamburg that decides on an Olympic application, but the German Olympic Sports Federation," von Beust told WELT ONLINE.
He referred to the expected decision of the Sportbund at the beginning of December on an application by Munich for the Winter Games 2018.
"This preference means for us that we will have no realistic chances in the period we have planned so far until 2028."
Nevertheless, the dream is not dreamed out for all time, von Beust said.
The Hamburger Sportbund (HSB) regretted the statements of Beusts.
This gives the impression that Hamburg no longer wants to apply for the Olympic Games in the next 25 years.
"It is too early to block an opportunity by burying the plans as early as this time," HSB President Günter Ploß said on Wednesday.
A renunciation of the application was also contrary to the statement of the head of the Senate on the occasion of a reception to the general assembly of the General German University Sports Federation.
There von Beust described Hamburg's bid for Universiade 2015 "not as a replacement" for the Olympic Games in Hamburg.
The university sports federation had only last week nominated Hamburg as an applicant city.
The SPD citizenry faction accused the Senate of failing to use valuable time for housing construction in Hamburg.
"The development of the Little Grasbrook has been delayed by the Senate for years because he put on the map of Olympia and excluded housing construction," SPD MPs Carola Veit and Jan Quast said.
While the SPD had always demanded integrated planning for the Little Grasbrook and a later residential use, the CDU had unilaterally relied on the "event factor".
The application for the 2012 Summer Olympics sparked great enthusiasm in Hamburg at the time.
Hamburg, however, had failed Leipzig.
They'ren't Warhol's boxes
Those who combine the terms Sweden and wooden box think of Ikea, but not Andy Warhol.
Or is it?
Currently, in Sweden, fabricated wooden boxes cast dark shadows on Warhol's catalogue of works.
A majority of ninety-four Brillo boxes of the "type Stockholm" listed there are counterfeits.
After a few months ago the Swedish newspaper "Expressen" has stoked doubts about the authenticity of Andy Warhol's Brillo boxes of the year 1968, internal investigations at the Moderna Museet in Stockholm now come to a sobering result: The six "Brillo-Boxes", which the former museum director Pontus Hultén bequeathed to the Moderna Museet in 1995 as a donation, are copies - and arguably also the majority
Prehistory is nested, a confusive concatenation of artistic and curatorial productions.
In 1964, Warhol created his first "brillo-boxes" in New York, painted wooden boxes based on the red-blue-and-white design of cardboard boxes of soap powder "Brillo."
An exhibition of his works at Moderna Museet in 1968 - with Warhol's consensus - turned hundreds of brillo boxes out of cardboard: these did not come (for cost reasons) from Warhol, but were delivered directly from the Brillo cardboard factory.
They were not works of art, but their role models, mere exhibits.
At the same time, Warhol probably granted permission in 1968 to produce a few Brillo wooden boxes in Sweden, which, however, were not exhibited at Moderna Museet.
Eyewitnesses now speak of around fifteen boxes, which at that time were justifiably incorporated into the history of art and auction as authorized original Andy-Warhol-Brillo-Brillo boxes with the designation "Typ Stockholm".
Some of them have scored more than 100 000 dollars at auctions.
The problem now is that the legendary museum director Pontus Hultén later falsely claimed that around a hundred wooden boxes had been produced and exhibited in Sweden in 1968, which Warhol then left to him.
In fact, Hultén's 105 boxes weren't produced until 1990 in Malmö for an exhibition in St. Petersburg.
At that time, Warhol had already been dead for three years.
Lars Byström, chief conservator at Moderna Museet, has examined, in addition to the six Brillo-Boxes owned in the museum, three of which were made in Stockholm in 1968: their pressed chipboards were first primed and sanded down, after which white oil paint was applied with a brush.
The structure of the copies from 1990, on the other hand, reveals that the paint was applied directly to the press chipboard with a painter's roll without a primer - in addition, it is not oil paint, but acrylic paint.
"These boxes weren't authorized by the artist and should be removed from the official list of Andy Warhol's Brillo-Boxes," said Lars Nittve, director of the Moderna Museet: "If the 'Andy Warhol Art Authentication Board' fails to come to a different conclusion, the Moderna Museet will classify the six boxes in its collection as copies/exhibition material."
Already in July, of course, the Authentication Board has announced to investigate the "serious accusations", which are made in matters of the soap powder boxes.
Since then, however, people have shrouded themselves in relentless silence.
Rebelling soldiers in Manila give up
In the Philippines, rebeling soldiers have given up entrenching themselves in a hotel in Manila, demanding the overthrow of President Gloria Macapagal Arroyo.
He could not be responsible for bloodshedding, the leader Antonio Trillanes, who accused the president of corruption and electoral fraud, told reporters at the hotel.
Pro-government soldiers had previously stormed the hotel under tear gas use.
The roughly two-dozen soldiers had been entrenched in the building for hours.
Around half of them were on trial on Thursday because of a failed coup attempt in 2003 - among them trillanes.
He used the trial to suddenly settle with around 30 sympathizers.
The military polices, who were supposed to guard him, had apparently defected to him and joined his mutiny.
They invaded the Peninsula hotel and demanded the overthrow of President Gloria Macapagal Arroyo.
Loyal government forces cordoned off the district around the noble hotel.
The president convened an urgent meeting of the security cabinet.
National security adviser Norberto Gonzales condemned the soldiers' actions.
"People have had enough," Trillanes said.
"We're calling on people to join us."
General Danilo Lim, who formerly commanded the scout rangers' elite unit, said other army units wanted to join the march shortly.
Government critics had called for big demonstrations against the president for Friday.
Her government has been overshadowed by allegations of corruption for months.
Manila's financial district was already the main scene of the attempted mutiny of around 200 military personnel against Arroyo in 2003.
The uprising failed after just under 24 hours because of the army's refusal to join in.
Since 1986 there have been at least seven coup attempts against democratically elected governments in the Philippines.
United Internet becomes major shareholder at Versatel
United Internet has secured a stake of more than 20 per cent in telecommunications company Versatel.
The average price per share is 14.46 euros, as the company announced on Wednesday evening.
20.05 percent of the share capital is now held.
There had previously been speculation about a takeover.
The option to increase the share of Versatel is kept open.
With the move, they want to promote the consolidation of the German DSL market, United Internet said.
Previously on Wednesday, suspicions had already arisen about an entry, after Berenberg Bank increased its stake in the Düsseldorf network operator by a further 5 percent to 15.1 percent.
The money house considers the shares to be only a third party, it said on the financial market.
The day before, United Internet had already contributed almost 10 percent in the mobile phone company Drillisch.
As part of a capital increase, Drillisch has placed 17.4 million new shares worth 106.4 million euros.
5.15 million shares of this have been bought by United Internet for 31.4 million euros.
In addition, the companies have started discussions about marketing cooperation for DSL and mobile phone products.
"We are strengthening our partnership," explained a United Internet spokesman.
The two companies have set up the joint venture MSP to acquire competitor Freenet.
For now, the two companies want to settle with a ten-percent stake in Freenet and make no use of their right to acquire another 18.49 per centre of Freenet, United Internet said on Thursday.
However, the two companies kept all options open, in particular to further increase the stake in Freenet AG, it said.
Talks between United Internet and Freenet on a strategic partnership had ended in mid-November.
Freenet’s poker, which has been going on for months and is difficult to comprehensible, is part of the consolidation of the German telecoms market.
At the end of the third quarter, Freenet had 2.9 million mobile contract customers and 1.27 million DSL and full-link customers.
The holding company of United Internet and Drillisch so far held directly 6.01 percent of Freenet's shares.
It has now been agreed that Drillisch contributes another around four percent.
The right to buy the 18.49 per cent from the investment company Vatas had already been agreed in August.
